    <node id="HEAD AND NECK CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Head and neck cancer is a group of cancers that arise in the head or neck region, encompassing malignancies of the oral cavity, oropharynx, nasopharynx, larynx, hypopharynx, and nasal cavity/paranasal sinuses. This group includes mouth cancer, and its development is strongly associated with tobacco and alcohol use as key risk factors. The diagnosis and treatment of head and neck cancer can have wide-ranging effects on a patient's health and quality of life. Treatment may impact the jaw, teeth, and gums, as well as overall oral health. Beyond the oral cavity, it can also affect endocrine glands, hearing, nutrition, speech, swallowing, fertility, and overall performance status. Thus, head and neck cancer represents a complex set of diseases requiring comprehensive care that addresses both the tumor and the multiple functional systems that may be compromised by the disease and its management.
Relationships:
  - Source: MOUTH CANCER
  - Target: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Description: Mouth cancer is a type of head and neck cancer. It belongs to the broader category of head and neck cancers, which includes several distinct cancer types. Thus, mouth cancer is one of the several cancers grouped under the head and neck cancer classification.
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: MOUTH CANCER
  - Description: Mouth cancer is a type of head and neck cancer, listed as cancer of the oral cavity
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: DENTAL EXAM
  - Description: Head and neck cancer treatment can significantly affect the jaw, teeth, and gums, making dental health a critical part of patient care. Because of these potential impacts, a dental exam is highly important for individuals undergoing or preparing for treatment. Regular dental cleanings and checkups are essential to monitor and maintain oral health, as head and neck cancer therapies—such as radiation or surgery—can compromise the structures of the mouth and jaw. Dental exams help identify and manage complications early, reducing the risk of serious dental issues during and after cancer treatment. Thus, for head and neck cancer patients, consistent dental care is a vital component of overall treatment and long-term wellbeing.
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: DENTIST
  - Description: It is important to tell your dentist about head and neck cancer</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
