    <node id="FAMILY HISTORY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Family history is the health history of a person’s blood relatives—particularly close blood relatives—including information about cancers and other diseases such as heart disease, cancer, and diabetes. It is gathered through a process in which the care team asks about the health conditions of these relatives. Family history is used to assess inherited risk, helping clinicians identify whether a person may have a higher chance of developing certain conditions due to genetic or familial factors. In the context of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), family history is specifically used during evaluation, as having affected close blood relatives can increase a person’s risk of developing CLL. Overall, family history serves as an important tool for understanding inherited health risks and guiding care decisions.

Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Family history of cancer may make genetic testing important
  - Source: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Target: GENETIC TESTING
  - Description: Family history of cancer may lead to genetic testing

