    <node id="GENETIC TEST">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">A genetic test is a medical test used to identify abnormal changes, known as mutations, in genes. These changes can indicate an inherited risk for certain cancers, such as inherited cancer risk. In the context of prostate cancer care, genetic testing is divided into two basic types: germline testing and molecular biomarker testing. Germline testing looks for inherited gene mutations, while molecular biomarker testing analyzes the tumor itself to detect genetic alterations that can guide treatment decisions. Together, these approaches help assess cancer risk, prognosis, and potential targeted therapies.
Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
  - Target: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: Genetic testing is used in prostate cancer care to assess cancer risk, prognosis, and potential targeted therapies.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
  - Target: PATIENT
  - Description: The patient receives genetic testing.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
  - Target: MOLECULAR BIOMARKER TESTING
  - Description: Molecular biomarker testing is a type of genetic testing that analyzes the tumor itself.</data>
