    <node id="ANAL CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Anal cancer, also known as anal canal cancer, is an uncommon cancer of the digestive system. It most often originates in the squamous cells that line the anal canal or the perianal region. As the cancer being treated, anal cancer can be classified as stage I, II, or III, and the standard treatment approach involves chemoradiation.
Relationships:
  - Source: SISCCA
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: SISCCA is the earliest form of anal cancer
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: ANAL CANAL CANCER
  - Description: Anal canal cancer is a form of anal cancer
  - Source: ANAL SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Anal squamous cell carcinoma is invasive anal cancer, more advanced than SISCCA
  - Source: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING (MRI)
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: MRI is recommended to assess anal cancer extent and lymph node involvement
  - Source: ANAL CANAL
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Anal cancer arises in the anal canal
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: HUMAN PAPILLOMAVIRUS INFECTION
  - Description: Long-term HPV infection is the risk factor most strongly linked with anal cancer
  - Source: HEMORRHOIDS
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Anal cancer symptoms may be incorrectly attributed to hemorrhoids
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: HIV INFECTION
  - Description: HIV infection weakens the immune system, making it harder to clear HPV infection and increasing anal cancer risk
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: ORGAN TRANSPLANT
  - Description: Taking immunosuppressants after an organ transplant is a risk factor for anal cancer
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: ANAL SEX
  - Description: A history of anal sex is listed as a risk factor for anal cancer
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: DIAGNOSIS AND TREATMENT PLANNING
  - Description: Diagnosis and treatment planning is part of managing anal cancer
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: ANAL DYSPLASIA
  - Description: Anal dysplasia is a precancerous condition that may develop into anal cancer
  - Source: RECTAL BLEEDING
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Rectal bleeding is the most common symptom of anal cancer
  - Source: HIGH-RESOLUTION ANOSCOPY
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: High-resolution anoscopy can identify anal cancer and anal dysplasia
  - Source: BIOPSY
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: A biopsy sample may provide the diagnosis of anal cancer
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Radiation therapy is typically given Monday through Friday for 5 to 6 weeks as part of chemoradiation for anal cancer
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: CANCER
  - Description: Anal cancer is a form of cancer
  - Source: CHEMORADIATION
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Chemoradiation is the recommended primary treatment for non-metastatic anal canal cancer and is used for anal cancer
  - Source: DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Digital rectal exam is important for diagnosis, staging, and follow-up of anal cancer
  - Source: LYMPH NODES
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Anal cancer can spread to lymph nodes in the groin and pelvis
  - Source: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: PET may be combined with CT or MRI to detect cancer spread
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Chemotherapy regimens are recommended for use with radiation therapy to treat stage I, II, and III anal cancer
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: People with anal cancer are encouraged to consider enrolling in a clinical trial
  - Source: METASTASIS
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Anal cancer can metastasize through lymphatics or blood to distant areas
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: CT SCAN
  - Description: CT scan is used during simulation to image the anal cancer sites
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Surveillance is performed to find early signs that anal cancer may have returned
  - Source: RECURRENCE
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Anal cancer may return after a cancer-free period, which is called recurrence
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: STAGING
  - Description: Staging is part of the assessment and guide for anal cancer
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: Smoking is listed as a risk factor for anal cancer
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA
  - Description: Anal squamous cell carcinoma is a common form of anal cancer
  - Source: INTENSITY-MODULATED RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: IMRT is the preferred radiation method for treating anal cancer
  - Source: COMPLETE REMISSION
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Anal cancer can be classified as being in complete remission when no signs of cancer are found
  - Source: HPV INFECTION
