    <node id="ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE">
      <data key="d3">7</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Active surveillance is a management and monitoring approach used for certain early-stage or low-risk cancers, most commonly prostate cancer and thyroid cancer. In the context of prostate cancer, active surveillance is a strategy for low-risk or early-stage disease in which patients undergo regular tests—such as PSA blood tests, digital rectal exams, and imaging—and avoid immediate treatment unless the cancer shows signs of growth or progression. Treatment is deliberately delayed, and intervention is initiated only if the cancer changes enough to require it. This approach typically involves more than one biopsy over time, including a confirmatory biopsy within the first year, to ensure the cancer remains low-risk and to detect any changes early. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) may also be used to help determine whether active surveillance is an appropriate option for a given patient, by providing detailed images of the prostate and aiding in risk assessment.

Active surveillance is also applied to some very-low-risk thyroid cancers. In this setting, it involves closely monitoring the cancer over time rather than performing surgery right away. The goal is to avoid unnecessary treatment and its potential side effects while ensuring that any significant progression is caught promptly. As with prostate cancer, active surveillance in thyroid cancer relies on regular follow-up examinations and imaging.

Overall, active surveillance is not “doing nothing”; it is an active, structured monitoring program designed to balance the benefits of avoiding immediate treatment against the risk of disease progression. It is most suitable for patients whose cancers are unlikely to become life-threatening in the near term, and it requires consistent adherence to follow-up schedules. By incorporating regular testing, repeat biopsies, and advanced imaging where appropriate, active surveillance allows patients to maintain quality of life while keeping the option of curative treatment available if the cancer ever progresses. This approach represents a shared decision-making process between the patient and the healthcare team, based on individual risk factors and preferences.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: Active surveillance is a management approach for low-risk or early-stage prostate cancer.
  - Source: ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Active surveillance involves more than one biopsy over time.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
