    <node id="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The Cancer Information Service (CIS) is a service of the National Cancer Institute (NCI), part of the U.S. National Institutes of Health. It serves as NCI's comprehensive cancer information and clinical trial referral service, helping people find and learn about clinical trials while also answering cancer-related questions. The CIS can be reached by phone at 1-800-4-CANCER (1-800-422-6237) and through the NCI website at cancer.gov/contact. As an integral part of the National Cancer Institute, the CIS provides accurate, up-to-date cancer information and assists individuals in locating and understanding clinical trial options.
Relationships:
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Description: Both the Cancer Information Service and NCCN are cancer information resources for patients and can help connect patients to treatment options and clinical trials.
  - Source: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: The Cancer Information Service assists individuals in locating appropriate clinical trials, including providing guidance on how to find them. It also answers cancer-related questions and addresses specific inquiries about clinical trials, such as their purpose, procedures, and eligibility. Additionally, the service offers contact information for further assistance, ensuring that individuals have access to the support they need when seeking information about cancer and clinical trial participation.
  - Source: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Target: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Description: The National Cancer Institute (NCI) operates the Cancer Information Service (CIS), which is an integral part of the Institute. The CIS functions as NCI's dedicated service for providing cancer information and clinical trial assistance. It helps both patients and the general public find relevant clinical trials and offers detailed information about clinical trial options. As a core component of the National Cancer Institute, the Cancer Information Service serves as a reliable resource for those seeking guidance on cancer-related topics and access to clinical trial information.
  - Source: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Description: CIS helps people find clinical trials
CIS helps people find clinical trials and provides cancer information by phone
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Target: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Description: CIS helps people find clinical trials and answers questions about them
  - Source: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Target: UNITED STATES
  - Description: The Cancer Information Service is a U.S.-based entity that operates as part of the National Cancer Institute, which is also based in the United States. As a service of this institute, it is situated within the broader organizational framework of the U.S. National Cancer Institute, reflecting its role in providing cancer-related information and support within the United States.
  - Source: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Target: U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE
  - Description: Both are resources that help patients find clinical trials; NLM provides clinicaltrials.gov and CIS offers direct assistance
  - Source: U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE
  - Target: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
  - Description: The U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE (NLM) and the CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE (CIS) are both organizations that help people find clinical trials. NLM maintains ClinicalTrials.gov, a comprehensive online database of clinical studies, while CIS offers direct, personal assistance to individuals seeking information about clinical trial options. Together, they provide complementary support: NLM supplies the searchable public resource, and CIS delivers one-on-one guidance to help users navigate the process.</data>
