    <node id="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is a medical organization that created the widely used cancer staging system to describe the extent of cancer in the body. Its system is designed to determine how much cancer is present, where it is located, and what subtype it is, thereby guiding prognosis and treatment decisions. The AJCC developed the TNM staging system, which evaluates three key components: tumor size and extent (T), lymph node involvement (N), and distant metastasis (M). In addition to TNM scores, the AJCC staging system incorporates tumor grade and, for certain cancers, biomarker testing—such as hormone receptor and HER2 status in breast cancer—to refine prognosis and guide therapy.

The AJCC staging system includes both clinical and pathologic staging and assigns numbered stages (e.g., stage I, II, III, IV) that represent the overall cancer burden. These numbered stages are used across multiple cancer types. For example, in mouth cancer, the AJCC system defines TNM and numbered stages; in colon cancer, it combines T, N, and M scores to stage the disease; in anal cancer and perianal tumors, it uses the TNM framework; and it also developed one of the staging systems for ovarian cancer, known as the AJCC staging system. In breast cancer, the AJCC system uses tumor size, lymph node involvement, metastasis, tumor grade, and hormone receptor/HER2 status to predict outcomes and inform treatment.

Overall, the American Joint Committee on Cancer serves as the authoritative organization for cancer staging, providing a standardized method for clinicians to describe cancer extent, location, and subtype, and to communicate prognosis across different malignancies. Its staging framework is integral to cancer diagnosis, treatment planning, and research, ensuring consistent classification for a broad range of cancers.
Relationships:
  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS
  - Description: The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) and the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) have both developed staging systems for ovarian cancer. The two systems are similar in structure and intent, providing standardized frameworks for classifying the extent of the disease. Although both staging systems are recognized and used in clinical practice, the FIGO system is employed most often. This reflects its widespread acceptance and practical utility in the management and communication of ovarian cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">3</data>
