    <node id="BREAST CANCER">
      <data key="d3">14</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Breast cancer is a disease that starts in the cells of the breast. It often begins in the milk ducts or lobules and can spread into surrounding tissue. Breast cancer can be invasive, meaning it has the ability to spread through the lymphatic system or blood to other parts of the body. Most breast cancers are carcinomas, with the common types being ductal carcinoma and lobular carcinoma. Anyone can develop breast cancer, regardless of gender or other factors. Testing is performed on the cancer to help determine an appropriate treatment plan.
Relationships:
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: Mammograms are used to image and screen for breast cancer.
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: BREAST CANCER SCREENING
  - Description: Breast cancer screening aims to detect breast cancer early, when it can be treated more successfully.
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: BREAST EXAM
  - Description: Breast exams help find breast cancer at earlier, more treatable stages.
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: Diagnostic mammograms are used when symptoms such as a lump, pain, or nipple changes may indicate breast cancer.
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: BREAST CARCINOMA
  - Description: Breast carcinoma is a carcinoma of the breast and is closely related to breast cancer.
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: REGULAR SCREENING
  - Description: Regular screening helps find breast cancer early.
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: MAMMOGRAM SCREENING
  - Description: Mammogram screening detects breast cancer.
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
  - Target: SCREENING MAMMOGRAM
  - Description: Screening mammograms are performed routinely to look for breast cancer when there are no signs or symptoms.</data>
