Description: CANCER STAGING is the process of describing the extent of cancer in the body, including the size and location of the tumor, lymph node involvement, and whether the cancer has spread (metastasis) to distant sites. It also incorporates additional prognostic factors such as tumor grade, hormone receptor status, and HER2 status. Staging is used to guide prognosis and treatment decisions, helping clinicians determine the most appropriate therapeutic approach.

Cancer staging is performed using standardized staging systems developed by organizations such as the AJCC (American Joint Committee on Cancer) and FIGO (International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics). For ovarian cancer specifically, the FIGO system is the one most often used. These systems provide a common language for describing the extent of disease, ensuring consistency in diagnosis, treatment planning, and communication among healthcare providers.

The staging process also determines whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes or other parts of the body. For example, in anal cancer, Stage I and II cancers have not invaded nearby lymph nodes, and staging helps determine whether local excision—surgical removal of the tumor and a small margin of surrounding tissue—is appropriate. This illustrates how staging directly influences treatment options based on the precise extent of the disease.

Overall, cancer staging is a critical component of oncology practice. It involves describing the size, location, lymph node involvement, and spread of cancer, and it is essential for predicting patient outcomes, selecting treatments, and comparing results across clinical studies. By integrating multiple disease characteristics, staging provides a comprehensive picture of each patient’s cancer, enabling personalized and evidence-based care.

Relationships: (None listed in the provided data)

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) characterized by the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene. This gene produces the BCR::ABL protein, which drives the disease. Treatment for this subtype specifically aims to stop the activity of the BCR::ABL protein and typically involves an intensive combination of systemic therapies.
Relationships:
  - Source: BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL
  - Description: These are distinct B-ALL subtypes classified by BCR::ABL1 status and are discussed in separate chapters of the NCCN guidelines.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL
  - Description: B-ALL (B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature B lymphocytes. BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-ALL defined by a specific genetic abnormality: the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, resulting from a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 (the Philadelphia chromosome). This genetic alteration drives the leukemia and has important implications for diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment. As a subtype of B-ALL, BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL shares the general features of B-ALL but is set apart by this molecular marker, making targeted therapies particularly relevant for this form of the disease.

---</data>
