    <node id="BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL is a subtype of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL). It is recognized as the most common type of B-ALL. For this subtype, participation in a clinical trial is considered the preferred treatment approach.
Relationships:
  - Source: BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL
  - Description: These are distinct B-ALL subtypes classified by BCR::ABL1 status and are discussed in separate chapters of the NCCN guidelines.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL
  - Description: B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) encompasses several subtypes, one of which is BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL. This subtype is defined by the absence of the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, yet it shares a gene expression profile similar to BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL, hence the term "BCR::ABL1-like." It is recognized as a distinct subtype of B-ALL and is also the most common type of B-ALL overall. Thus, BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL represents the most frequently diagnosed form of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, characterized by its specific molecular and clinical features.

---</data>
