    <node id="B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) that arises from B-cell lymphocytes. It is one of the two main types of ALL, the other being T-ALL (T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia), and it is notably more common than T-ALL. B-ALL encompasses several distinct subtypes, including mature B-cell ALL and forms defined by genetic markers, such as BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL (also known as Philadelphia chromosome-positive ALL) and BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL. As a disease of B-cell precursors, B-ALL is characterized by the malignant proliferation of immature B cells in the bone marrow, which can interfere with normal blood cell production. Overall, B-ALL represents a major category of childhood and adult leukemia, and its classification into genetic subtypes is important for prognosis and treatment planning.
Relationships:
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL
  - Description: B-ALL (B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature B lymphocytes. BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-ALL defined by a specific genetic abnormality: the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, resulting from a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 (the Philadelphia chromosome). This genetic alteration drives the leukemia and has important implications for diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment. As a subtype of B-ALL, BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL shares the general features of B-ALL but is set apart by this molecular marker, making targeted therapies particularly relevant for this form of the disease.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL
  - Description: B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) encompasses several subtypes, one of which is BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL. This subtype is defined by the absence of the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, yet it shares a gene expression profile similar to BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL, hence the term "BCR::ABL1-like." It is recognized as a distinct subtype of B-ALL and is also the most common type of B-ALL overall. Thus, BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL represents the most frequently diagnosed form of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, characterized by its specific molecular and clinical features.

---</data>
