    <node id="BURKITT LEUKEMIA">
      <data key="d3">3</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Burkitt leukemia, also called mature B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), is a rare subtype of B-cell ALL. It is essentially the same disease as Burkitt lymphoma, sharing the same underlying pathology, but it is distinguished by its clinical presentation and treatment approach. Specifically, Burkitt leukemia is treated differently from standard B-ALL, despite being classified as a form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia. This distinction is important because, although it falls under the ALL umbrella, its management follows protocols more akin to Burkitt lymphoma rather than typical B-ALL treatment regimens. In summary, Burkitt leukemia is a rare, mature B-cell ALL that is biologically equivalent to Burkitt lymphoma yet requires a unique therapeutic strategy compared to conventional B-ALL.
Relationships:
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: BURKITT LEUKEMIA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia is treated differently from B-ALL
  - Source: B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: BURKITT LEUKEMIA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia is mature B-cell ALL, a rare form whose treatment differs from standard B-ALL. Burkitt leukemia, also called mature B-cell ALL, is treated differently from standard B-ALL.
  - Source: B-CELL ALL
  - Target: BURKITT LEUKEMIA
  - Description: Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of B-cell ALL, also called mature B-cell ALL.</data>
