    <node id="NEEDLE BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Needle biopsy is a medical procedure in which a needle is used to extract a sample of tissue or fluid from the body for diagnostic testing. It is often performed on a lymph node, allowing pathologists to examine the sample for abnormalities such as infection or cancer. The procedure is also known by another name: fine-needle aspiration (FNA), reflecting its minimally invasive nature and use of a thin needle to collect cellular material. When required, a needle biopsy may be carried out by a breast diagnostic radiologist, particularly in cases involving suspicious breast findings. In this context, the radiologist uses imaging guidance to precisely place the needle and obtain an adequate sample for evaluation. Thus, needle biopsy is a versatile and commonly used diagnostic tool that can be performed on various body sites, with a lymph node being one example, and is closely associated with fine-needle aspiration and radiological expertise.
Relationships: (None listed in the provided data)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
