    <node id="GENETIC CANCER RISK TESTING">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Genetic cancer risk testing is a type of testing that evaluates a person's inherited risk of developing cancer. It is also listed as a test option under the category of testing for ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS). The testing looks for inherited gene changes, specifically germline mutations, that may increase an individual's likelihood of developing cancer. To perform the test, a sample is collected from the person—typically through blood, saliva obtained by spitting into a cup, or a cheek swab—and analyzed for these inherited mutations. This testing helps identify individuals who may be at higher genetic risk for certain cancers, allowing for more informed medical decisions and potential preventive measures.
Relationships: (No direct relationships listed in the provided data)</data>
