    <node id="MOUTH CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Mouth cancer, also known as oral cancer or oral cavity cancer, is a malignant condition that develops in the oral cavity and is classified as a type of head and neck cancer. It can arise in various parts of the mouth, including the lips, gums (gingiva), buccal mucosa (the inner lining of the cheeks), the front two-thirds of the tongue, the hard palate, the alveolar ridge, the floor of the mouth, and the retromolar trigone (the area behind the wisdom teeth). The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides detailed information and clinical guidance on this disease. Mouth cancer is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of cells in these oral structures, and its management typically involves a multidisciplinary approach.
Relationships:
  - Source: MOUTH CANCER
  - Target: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Description: Mouth cancer is a type of head and neck cancer. It belongs to the broader category of head and neck cancers, which includes several distinct cancer types. Thus, mouth cancer is one of the several cancers grouped under the head and neck cancer classification.
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: MOUTH CANCER
  - Description: Mouth cancer is a type of head and neck cancer, listed as cancer of the oral cavity
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: MOUTH CANCER
  - Description: NCCN provides more information on mouth cancer at NCCN.org/patientguidelines
  - Source: MOUTH CANCER
  - Target: CANCER
  - Description: Mouth cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the oral cavity.
  - Source: MOUTH CANCER
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Cancer treatment for mouth cancer is based on tumor location and cancer stage
  - Source: MOUTH CANCER
  - Target: SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA
  - Description: Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is the most common type of mouth cancer. Mouth cancer encompasses various malignancies, and among them, squamous cell carcinoma is the most frequently diagnosed form. Both statements confirm that SCC, also referred to as squamous cell carcinoma, represents the predominant subtype of mouth cancer.
  - Source: MOUTH CANCER
  - Target: CUTANEOUS SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA OF THE VERMILION LIP
  - Description: Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma of the vermilion lip is explicitly excluded from the mouth cancer book, distinguishing it from cancers of the mucosal lip and oral cavity.
  - Source: MOUTH CANCER
  - Target: ORAL CANCER
  - Description: Mouth cancer is also called oral cancer</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
