    <node id="SOURCE: METASTATIC CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread from its original site to distant organs or tissues. In this context, biomarker testing plays a crucial role in guiding treatment decisions. For metastatic cancer, a tumor may be tested for specific biomarkers to help determine the most effective therapeutic approach. Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumor tissue or blood for genetic mutations, protein expression, or other molecular characteristics that can predict how a cancer will respond to certain treatments. This is particularly relevant for metastatic cervical cancer, where PD-L1 and biomarker testing is recommended. Testing for PD-L1 expression and other biomarkers in metastatic cervical cancer helps determine whether certain targeted therapies or immunotherapies, such as checkpoint inhibitors, may be viable options for the patient. By identifying the presence or absence of these biomarkers, oncologists can personalize treatment plans, potentially improving outcomes and avoiding ineffective therapies. Overall, biomarker testing in metastatic cancer, including cervical cancer, is an essential tool for precision medicine, enabling more tailored and effective treatment strategies based on the unique molecular profile of each patient's tumor.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">    Target: BIOMARKER TESTING
