    <node id="OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Ovarian transposition, also medically termed oophoropexy, is a surgical procedure that moves one or both ovaries out of the range of a radiation beam in order to protect them during radiation therapy. This procedure is specifically used to shield the ovaries from radiation exposure, thereby helping to preserve ovarian function. In premenopausal women with squamous cell carcinoma, ovarian transposition may reduce the risk of radiation-induced menopause. Overall, the surgery aims to safeguard fertility and hormonal function by relocating the ovaries away from the targeted radiation field.
Relationships:
  - Source: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Target: RADIATION-INDUCED MENOPAUSE
  - Description: Ovarian transposition may reduce or prevent radiation-induced menopause.
  - Source: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Target: OVARIES
  - Description: Ovarian transposition moves one or both ovaries out of the radiation beam range.
  - Source: OVARIAN TISSUE BANKING
  - Target: OVARIAN TRANSPOSITION
  - Description: Both ovarian tissue banking and ovarian transposition are described as fertility preservation options for people who may want children after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
