    <node id="THUS, COLON CANCER IS A MAJOR COMPLICATION OF THIS INHERITED SYNDROME, WITH FAP SERVING AS A DIRECT PRECURSOR CONDITION.">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">FAP causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">  - Source: POLYPS
  - Target: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS
  - Description: FAP causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum.

ENTITY: CARNEY COMPLEX
Description: Carney complex is a hereditary condition that may increase the risk of developing an adrenal tumor.
Relationships:
  - Source: CARNEY COMPLEX
  - Target: ADRENAL TUMORS
  - Description: Carney complex is a hereditary risk factor for developing an adrenal tumor.

ENTITY: ADRENOCORTICAL CARCINOMA
Description: Adrenocortical carcinoma is a rare adrenal cancer that starts in the adrenal cortex. It can be functioning or nonfunctioning, can occur at any age, and is most likely to affect children ages 5 and under and adults in their 40s and 50s.
Relationships:
  - Source: ADRENOCORTICAL CARCINOMA
  - Target: ADRENAL TUMORS
  - Description: Adrenocortical carcinoma is a type of adrenal tumor.

ENTITY: LYNCH SYNDROME
Description: Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition caused by mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes. It is a hereditary disorder that significantly increases the risk of developing several types of cancer, most notably colon cancer, as well as uterine (endometrial), ovarian, and other cancers. Lynch syndrome can also cause hereditary ovarian cancer and may increase the risk of developing an adrenal tumor. Because of its strong hereditary nature and elevated cancer risks, Lynch syndrome is recognized as a factor that may make germline genetic testing appropriate for affected individuals and their families.
Relationships:
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Target: ADRENAL TUMORS
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is a hereditary risk factor for developing an adrenal tumor.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
