    <node id="ADENOMA">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Adenoma is a broad term for benign epithelial tumors that can arise in various organs. In the colon, adenoma is the most common type of polyp. While adenomas themselves are noncancerous growths, certain types—particularly colonic adenomas—have the potential to become invasive colon cancer over many years, which is why they are often monitored or removed. In the adrenal gland, adenoma—also known as adrenocortical adenoma—is the most common type of adrenal gland tumor. It is a noncancerous tumor that may be either functioning or nonfunctioning: functioning adenomas produce excess hormones, while nonfunctioning adenomas do not. Depending on its size, hormone production, and whether it causes symptoms, an adrenal adenoma may not require treatment. Small, asymptomatic, nonfunctioning adrenal adenomas typically require no intervention and are often managed with observation. Thus, the term "adenoma" encompasses a range of benign tumors, with clinical significance varying by location; colonic adenomas carry a risk of malignant transformation, whereas adrenal adenomas are generally benign and frequently incidental findings that may not need any treatment.
Relationships:
  - Source: ADENOMA
  - Target: ADRENAL TUMORS
  - Description: Adenoma is the most common type of adrenal gland tumor.
  - Source: ADENOMA
  - Target: ALDOSTERONOMA
  - Description: Aldosteronoma is an adenoma that produces too much aldosterone.
  - Source: ADENOMA
  - Target: CUSHING SYNDROME
  - Description: Cushing syndrome is caused by an adenoma that produces too much cortisol.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: ADENOMA
  - Description: Adenomas can become invasive colon cancer over time.
  - Source: POLYPS
  - Target: ADENOMA
  - Description: Adenoma is the most common type of polyp.
  - Source: ADENOMA
  - Target: ADENOCARCINOMA
  - Description: Cancer that forms in an adenoma is called adenocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">6</data>
