    <node id="POLYPECTOMY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps. This procedure is used to extract abnormal growths, known as polyps, from the lining of the colon or rectum. By removing these polyps during the endoscopic examination, polypectomy helps prevent potential complications, such as the development of colorectal cancer, and allows for pathological analysis of the removed tissue. The procedure is typically conducted by a gastroenterologist or surgeon using specialized instruments passed through the colonoscope, and it is considered a routine and minimally invasive intervention. Overall, polypectomy is an essential component of colon cancer screening and prevention, enabling both diagnosis and treatment in a single session.
**Relationships:**
  - None listed in the provided data</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
