    <node id="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d3">3</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging modality that merges positron emission tomography (PET) with computed tomography (CT). It uses the radiotracer fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), also known as F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose, to detect cancer by showing glucose uptake in cells. Patients must not eat or drink for at least 4 hours before the scan. This imaging test is used to evaluate cancer and can identify malignant involvement in primary sites, lymph nodes, and/or distant sites. It is particularly useful when other imaging results are unclear, and the scan can be performed at the same time as a diagnostic CT. In specific clinical contexts, FDG-PET/CT is used as a follow-up imaging scan from the neck to the midthigh, usually performed 3 to 6 months after treatment for certain cervical cancer stages. Additionally, it may be used when leukemia outside the blood and bone marrow is suspected. The combination of PET and CT provides both metabolic and anatomic information, aiding in the detection and evaluation of cancer.
Relationships: (No relationships were provided in the data for this entity.)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: FDG-PET/CT
Description: FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging modality that merges positron emission tomography (PET) with computed tomography (CT). It uses the radiotracer fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), also known as F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose, to detect cancer by showing glucose uptake in cells. Patients must not eat or drink for at least 4 hours before the scan. This imaging test is used to evaluate cancer and can identify malignant involvement in primary sites, lymph nodes, and/or distant sites. It is particularly useful when other imaging results are unclear, and the scan can be performed at the same time as a diagnostic CT. In specific clinical contexts, FDG-PET/CT is used as a follow-up imaging scan from the neck to the midthigh, usually performed 3 to 6 months after treatment for certain cervical cancer stages. Additionally, it may be used when leukemia outside the blood and bone marrow is suspected. The combination of PET and CT provides both metabolic and anatomic information, aiding in the detection and evaluation of cancer.
Relationships: (No relationships were provided in the data for this entity.)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT SCAN">
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">PET/CT scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that combines a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with a computed tomography (CT) scan into a single examination. This combined imaging technique integrates PET and CT to provide both metabolic information and anatomical detail in one scan, producing more comprehensive and detailed cross-sectional images than either method could achieve alone.

The PET portion of the scan detects metabolic activity, showing how tissues and organs are functioning at a cellular level, while the CT component provides precise anatomical structure and localization. By fusing these two types of data, the PET/CT scan allows physicians to pinpoint areas of abnormal metabolic activity within their exact anatomical context. This makes it particularly valuable for oncology, where it is used to detect, characterize, and monitor various cancers. For example, a combined PET and CT scan can create detailed images to find cancer that has spread outside the bladder, and it can also identify cancer in the prostate as well as its spread to a pelvic lymph node. In this way, the procedure helps in staging cancers and guiding treatment decisions by revealing both the location and the metabolic activity of malignant lesions. The result is a more detailed and accurate image than a PET scan or CT scan performed separately, making PET/CT a powerful tool in modern medical diagnostics.
Relationships: (No relationships were provided in the data for this entity.)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: PET/CT SCAN
Description: PET/CT scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that combines a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with a computed tomography (CT) scan into a single examination. This combined imaging technique integrates PET and CT to provide both metabolic information and anatomical detail in one scan, producing more comprehensive and detailed cross-sectional images than either method could achieve alone.

The PET portion of the scan detects metabolic activity, showing how tissues and organs are functioning at a cellular level, while the CT component provides precise anatomical structure and localization. By fusing these two types of data, the PET/CT scan allows physicians to pinpoint areas of abnormal metabolic activity within their exact anatomical context. This makes it particularly valuable for oncology, where it is used to detect, characterize, and monitor various cancers. For example, a combined PET and CT scan can create detailed images to find cancer that has spread outside the bladder, and it can also identify cancer in the prostate as well as its spread to a pelvic lymph node. In this way, the procedure helps in staging cancers and guiding treatment decisions by revealing both the location and the metabolic activity of malignant lesions. The result is a more detailed and accurate image than a PET scan or CT scan performed separately, making PET/CT a powerful tool in modern medical diagnostics.
Relationships: (No relationships were provided in the data for this entity.)</data>
    </node>
