Description: Lymphoma is a cancer of lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell that plays a central role in the immune system. It is characterized by an abnormal overgrowth of these cells, which can accumulate in lymph nodes and other tissues. One specific form is primary CNS lymphoma, which arises in the central nervous system. In the context of treatment described here, high-dose chemotherapy is administered to kill lymphoma cells throughout the body during a procedure known as a stem cell rescue, which helps restore healthy blood-forming cells after the intensive therapy.
Relationships:
  - Source: LYMPHOMA
  - Target: PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA
  - Description: Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of lymphoma</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUROLOGICAL EXAM">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">A neurological exam is a test used to assess brain and nervous system function, particularly in diagnosing primary CNS lymphoma. It consists of simple tests that evaluate alertness, thinking, balance, coordination, reflexes, strength, hearing, and the senses of touch and smell, and it may also include an eye exam.
Relationships:
  - Source: NEUROLOGICAL EXAM
  - Target: EYE EXAM
  - Description: An eye exam may be performed as part of the neurological exam to check optic-nerve health.
  - Source: DIAGNOSIS
  - Target: NEUROLOGICAL EXAM
  - Description: A neurological exam is used in the diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma
  - Source: NEUROLOGICAL EXAM
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The care team uses neurological exam results together with imaging and other assessments.
  - Source: NEUROLOGICAL EXAM
  - Target: IMAGING
  - Description: Neurological symptoms found during an exam can prompt imaging to look for a brain tumor.
  - Source: NEUROLOGICAL EXAM
  - Target: PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA
  - Description: A neurological exam is one of the important tests to confirm primary CNS lymphoma</data>
