    <node id="ANOSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">8</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">ANOSCOPY is a medical procedure used to inspect the anal canal and lower rectum using an anoscope. It is typically performed as a surveillance exam during follow-up care for individuals treated for anal cancer, including during remission. The procedure is recommended every 6 to 12 months for a period of 3 years after treatment, when feasible. During follow-up, anoscopy is often combined with a digital rectal examination to ensure thorough assessment. In cases where treatment was limited to lymph nodes only, anoscopy is still recommended on the same schedule, every 6 to 12 months for 3 years; however, it may be omitted if the procedure causes significant pain, particularly after radiation therapy.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Both DREs and anoscopy are follow-up tests recommended after treatment for cancer in lymph nodes only
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Surveillance and anoscopy are closely related in post-treatment monitoring. Anoscopy is a recommended component of surveillance protocols when feasible. Specifically, after lymph node-only treatment, surveillance includes anoscopy performed every 6 to 12 months for a period of 3 years. Thus, the surveillance strategy incorporates regular anoscopic examinations to monitor for potential recurrence or complications, with the frequency and duration clearly defined in the context of lymph node-only treatment.
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: After radiation therapy, anoscopy may be impossible due to pain and can be omitted or performed less often
  - Source: PHYSICAL EXAM
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: The physical exam is performed together with anoscopy.
  - Source: FOLLOW-UP AND SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Follow-up and surveillance includes anoscopy.
  - Source: DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Digital rectal examination and anoscopy are recommended together during the follow-up exam.
  - Source: DRE
  - Target: ANOSCOPY
  - Description: Surveillance during remission includes DRE and anoscopy on different schedules
  - Source: ANOSCOPY
  - Target: LYMPH NODE EXAMS
  - Description: Both are surveillance exams performed during remission after anal cancer treatment</data>
