    <node id="RICHTER TRANSFORMATION">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Richter transformation is a rare complication of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) and small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL), in which the disease transforms into a more aggressive form. This condition can arise from CLL and may lead to more aggressive cancers, most commonly diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) or, less frequently, Hodgkin lymphoma. When Richter transformation occurs, a patient’s care team may inform them of the diagnosis, and it is described in Chapter 5 of the patient guide. That guide also includes information on treatment and supportive care for this condition. Overall, Richter transformation represents a serious and uncommon progression of CLL/SLL, requiring specialized management as outlined in the patient education materials.
Relationships:
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: RICHTER TRANSFORMATION
  - Description: The NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) Guidelines for Patients describe Richter transformation as a possible complication of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) that can lead to more aggressive lymphomas. Additionally, the NCCN Guidelines for Patients address caring for Richter transformation as part of living with CLL/SLL (chronic lymphocytic leukemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma). Together, these descriptions indicate that Richter transformation is recognized by the NCCN as both a significant CLL complication and an important aspect of ongoing patient care and disease management within the broader context of CLL/SLL.
  - Source: CANCER CARE TEAM
  - Target: RICHTER TRANSFORMATION
  - Description: The cancer care team tells the patient if they have Richter transformation and directs them to read Chapter 5.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: RICHTER TRANSFORMATION
