    <node id="COMPLETE REMISSION">
      <data key="d3">6</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">COMPLETE REMISSION is a clinical state or category assigned when initial follow-up testing finds no signs of cancer in the body. It represents the absence of detectable cancer after treatment and is determined during follow-up and surveillance. In the context of platinum-based chemotherapy, complete remission specifically means there is no evidence of cancer following treatment; if the cancer returns more than 6 months later, it is considered platinum-sensitive.
Relationships:
  - Source: CHEMORADIATION
  - Target: COMPLETE REMISSION
  - Description: Complete remission is a possible outcome that is evaluated after chemoradiation.
  - Source: COMPLETE REMISSION
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Anal cancer can be classified as being in complete remission when no signs of cancer are found
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: COMPLETE REMISSION
  - Description: A complete remission result leads to the recommended surveillance schedule described in the text
  - Source: RECURRENCE
  - Target: COMPLETE REMISSION
  - Description: Cancer can return after complete remission; if it returns more than 6 months later, the disease is considered platinum-sensitive.
  - Source: FOLLOW-UP AND SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: COMPLETE REMISSION
  - Description: Surveillance results determine whether the cancer is in complete remission.
  - Source: COMPLETE REMISSION
  - Target: PERSISTENT DISEASE
  - Description: Persistent disease may go into complete remission after monitoring for up to 6 months</data>
