    <node id="RECTAL CANCER">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Rectal cancer is a malignant disease that forms in the rectum, which is the final section of the large intestine. As a cancer of the rectum, it is the focus of dedicated patient education materials: the NCCN Guidelines for Patients specifically address this disease and its treatment. These guidelines provide evidence-based information on managing rectal cancer, covering topics such as diagnosis, treatment options, and supportive care. The staging of rectal cancer ranges from stage I to stage III, and this classification is determined by two key factors: the extent of tumor growth into the rectal wall and the involvement of nearby lymph nodes. In summary, rectal cancer is a distinct cancer type with established clinical guidance for patients, and its staging system—based on local invasion and lymphatic spread—helps define the appropriate treatment approach.
Relationships: None listed</data>
