    <node id="RADICAL CYSTECTOMY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Radical cystectomy is the most widely used surgical treatment for muscle-invasive bladder cancer. It is a major operation that involves the removal of the entire bladder, nearby lymph nodes, and sometimes other organs in the pelvis. Because it is a significant procedure, it requires a hospital stay and a recovery period that typically lasts from two to three months or longer.
Relationships:
  - Source: CYSTECTOMY
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: Radical cystectomy is a type of cystectomy that removes the entire bladder along with nearby lymph nodes and possibly other organs.
  - Source: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Target: BLADDER REMOVAL SURGERY
  - Description: Radical cystectomy is the most widely used surgery for muscle-invasive bladder cancer and is a form of bladder removal surgery.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: NCCN's patient education chapter describes radical cystectomy as the most common surgery for muscle-invasive bladder cancer.
  - Source: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Target: URINARY DIVERSION
  - Description: Radical cystectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the bladder. Urinary diversion is a complementary surgical procedure performed at the same time as radical cystectomy, designed to provide a new way for urine to leave the body. Together, these procedures ensure that, after the bladder is removed, a functional pathway for urine excretion is established. Urinary diversion is thus an integral part of the overall surgical management when radical cystectomy is performed.
  - Source: TRANSURETHRAL RESECTION OF BLADDER TUMOR (TURBT)
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: Both TURBT and radical cystectomy are surgical treatment options for bladder cancer; TURBT removes tumors from the bladder wall and radical cystectomy removes the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">5</data>
      <data key="d4">ENTITY: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
Description: Radical cystectomy is the most widely used surgical treatment for muscle-invasive bladder cancer. It is a major operation that involves the removal of the entire bladder, nearby lymph nodes, and sometimes other organs in the pelvis. Because it is a significant procedure, it requires a hospital stay and a recovery period that typically lasts from two to three months or longer.
Relationships:
  - Source: CYSTECTOMY
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: Radical cystectomy is a type of cystectomy that removes the entire bladder along with nearby lymph nodes and possibly other organs.
  - Source: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Target: BLADDER REMOVAL SURGERY
  - Description: Radical cystectomy is the most widely used surgery for muscle-invasive bladder cancer and is a form of bladder removal surgery.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: NCCN's patient education chapter describes radical cystectomy as the most common surgery for muscle-invasive bladder cancer.
  - Source: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Target: URINARY DIVERSION
  - Description: Radical cystectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the bladder. Urinary diversion is a complementary surgical procedure performed at the same time as radical cystectomy, designed to provide a new way for urine to leave the body. Together, these procedures ensure that, after the bladder is removed, a functional pathway for urine excretion is established. Urinary diversion is thus an integral part of the overall surgical management when radical cystectomy is performed.
  - Source: TRANSURETHRAL RESECTION OF BLADDER TUMOR (TURBT)
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: Both TURBT and radical cystectomy are surgical treatment options for bladder cancer; TURBT removes tumors from the bladder wall and radical cystectomy removes the bladder.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PROCTOSCOPY">
      <data key="d3">7</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Proctoscopy is a medical procedure that uses a proctoscope to visually examine the anus and rectum. It is often performed under anesthesia and may be needed to look for signs of cancer in the bowel. In addition to its diagnostic role, proctoscopy serves as a surveillance examination following transanal local excision. The recommended surveillance schedule includes examinations every 3 to 6 months for the first 2 years, followed by every 6 months for the next 3 years, covering a total period of 5 years after the procedure. This comprehensive approach helps monitor for recurrence or signs of cancer in the bowel.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: SURVEILLANCE
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Proctoscopy is a surveillance examination performed after transanal local excision
  - Source: TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Proctoscopy is recommended for 5 years after transanal local excision to monitor for recurrence
  - Source: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Target: TESTING
  - Description: Proctoscopy is a testing procedure used to look for signs of cancer in the bowel
  - Source: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Target: COLONOSCOPY
  - Description: Both are endoscopic surveillance procedures recommended after transanal local excision; proctoscopy is more frequent and colonoscopy is done at longer intervals
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Proctoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the anus and rectum, and it plays a role in evaluating certain cancers. Specifically, proctoscopy is used to look for signs of cancer in the bowel. In the context of cervical cancer, proctoscopy may be needed to look for signs of cervical cancer in the bowel. Additionally, proctoscopy may be used to check the anus and rectum for cervical cancer spread, helping to determine whether the cancer has metastasized to these areas. Thus, for cervical cancer patients, proctoscopy serves as a diagnostic tool to assess possible bowel involvement and local spread of the disease.
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Both are procedures used under anesthesia to examine organs for signs of cancer
  - Source: EXAMINATIONS UNDER ANESTHESIA
  - Target: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Description: Proctoscopy is one of the examinations that can be performed under anesthesia</data>
