    <node id="TUMOR REMOVAL">
      <data key="d3">6</data>
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">TUMOR REMOVAL is a surgical procedure used to treat bladder cancer. It involves resecting the tumor while leaving the rest of the bladder intact, a technique also known as a resection. The procedure is typically performed in an operating room to remove a tumor that has been identified during a cystoscopy. In cases of very small cancer growths, the removal can be done directly during the cystoscopy itself. Thus, tumor removal encompasses both operating-room procedures and minimally invasive removal during diagnostic cystoscopy, all aimed at excising cancerous tissue while preserving bladder function.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
