    <node id="TURBT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">TURBT, or transurethral resection of bladder tumor, is a surgical procedure used to diagnose and treat bladder cancer. During the procedure, a surgeon examines the bladder wall through the urethra and removes visible tumors. TURBT also provides a tissue sample of the bladder wall for diagnostic purposes, helping confirm the presence and type of cancer.

As a treatment event, TURBT is a key component of trimodal therapy, where it is used to remove all visible bladder cancer. After the initial TURBT procedure, follow-up treatments are typically scheduled to destroy any remaining cancer cells. These follow-up approaches may include intravesical therapy—delivering medication directly into the bladder—or chemotherapy, both of which are discussed in relation to TURBT as part of a comprehensive bladder cancer management plan.
Relationships:
  - Source: TRANSURETHRAL RESECTION OF BLADDER TUMOR (TURBT)
  - Target: RADICAL CYSTECTOMY
  - Description: Both TURBT and radical cystectomy are surgical treatment options for bladder cancer; TURBT removes tumors from the bladder wall and radical cystectomy removes the bladder.
  - Source: TRANSURETHRAL RESECTION OF BLADDER TUMOR (TURBT)
  - Target: BLADDER REMOVAL SURGERY
  - Description: TURBT and bladder removal surgery are both surgical procedures used to treat bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
