    <node id="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Intravesical therapy is a local treatment for bladder cancer in which liquid medication is placed directly into the bladder through a catheter. The medicine is slowly instilled into the bladder, allowing it to come into contact with the inner lining and destroy cancer cells, including those that are not visible or difficult to reach through surgical means. This therapy is typically administered after a procedure called transurethral resection of a bladder tumor (TURBT), and it is often used in combination with TURBT to eliminate remaining cancer cells and help prevent new tumors from forming. While intravesical therapy is effective for treating superficial bladder cancer, it can cause side effects such as inflammation of the bladder lining, frequent urination, pain or burning during urination, blood in the urine, and infection. Overall, intravesical therapy serves as a targeted, bladder-directed treatment that reduces the risk of recurrence after surgical removal of visible tumors.
Relationships:
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is a form of intravesical therapy.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy is a type of intravesical therapy.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG
  - Description: Intravesical BCG is a type of intravesical therapy.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: TURBT
  - Description: Intravesical therapy and TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumor) are two treatments commonly used together for bladder cancer. TURBT is a surgical procedure that removes visible tumors from the bladder lining. Intravesical therapy involves delivering anticancer drugs directly into the bladder. It is often performed in combination with TURBT, and its primary purpose is to destroy cancer cells that TURBT left behind, helping to reduce the risk of recurrence and eliminate residual disease. Together, these treatments provide a combined approach: TURBT removes the main tumor mass, while intravesical therapy targets remaining microscopic cancer cells.
  - Source: TURBT
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical therapy is given after TURBT to destroy any remaining cancer cells and prevent new cancer cell formation.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical therapy and chemotherapy are both cancer-fighting treatments, but they differ in how the drugs are administered. Intravesical therapy is a localized treatment used primarily for bladder cancer, in which medication is placed directly into the bladder through a catheter. Chemotherapy, in its common systemic form, involves cancer-fighting drugs given throughout the body by infusion, usually through a vein, so that the drugs travel in the bloodstream to reach cancer cells anywhere in the body. However, the two are closely related in the context of bladder cancer: chemotherapy is actually one of the two main intravesical therapies. This means that chemotherapy drugs can be administered intravesically—directly into the bladder—rather than systemically. In that form, it is known as intravesical chemotherapy. The other main intravesical therapy is typically immunotherapy (such as BCG), which is not chemotherapy. Thus, the apparent contradiction is resolved by distinguishing between two modes of chemotherapy: intravesical chemotherapy, where the drugs are placed directly into the bladder as a type of intravesical therapy, and systemic chemotherapy, where the drugs are infused throughout the body. Both intravesical therapy and chemotherapy involve cancer-fighting drugs, but intravesical therapy is always local to the bladder, while chemotherapy can be either local (intravesical) or systemic.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical therapy is a local treatment, whereas systemic therapy treats the whole body; the text contrasts these approaches.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Description: NCCN's patient education chapter lists intravesical therapy as a treatment option for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
