    <node id="WATCH AND WAIT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">"Watch and wait" is a medical management strategy used in two distinct clinical contexts, each involving deferral of immediate active treatment in favor of close monitoring.

In the context of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), watch and wait is a management stage in which the patient does not begin active treatment, aside from measures aimed at relieving symptoms. This approach is described in Chapter 3 of relevant medical literature. It is typically employed when the disease is stable or indolent, allowing patients to avoid treatment-related side effects until progression warrants intervention.

In the context of rectal cancer, watch and wait is a management option following chemoradiation, in which no immediate additional treatment is given. Instead, the patient is closely monitored for signs of residual or recurrent disease. This strategy may be considered when a complete clinical response is achieved after chemoradiation, thereby avoiding surgery or further immediate therapy.

Thus, while the term "watch and wait" refers to a shared principle of active surveillance rather than immediate treatment, its application differs by disease: in CLL it is an initial management phase, whereas in rectal cancer it is a post-chemoradiation response assessment strategy. Both approaches prioritize monitoring and symptom-directed care over proactive intervention, with the goal of reserving treatment for when it becomes clinically necessary.
Relationships: (None listed in the data)</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
