    <node id="COLONOSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine, inspect, and view the colon and rectum. It is a key diagnostic and therapeutic tool: most colon cancers are found during colonoscopy, and polyps can be removed during the procedure, typically using a technique called polypectomy. This allows for both detection and prevention of colorectal cancer in many cases.

In addition to its diagnostic role, colonoscopy is commonly used as a surveillance examination after treatment for colorectal cancer. For patients who have undergone transanal local excision surgery, a colonoscopy is recommended 1 year after the procedure. If the results are good, the next surveillance colonoscopy is recommended in 3 years, and thereafter every 5 years. For patients with stage 1 colorectal cancer, a surveillance colonoscopy is also recommended 1 year after surgery, as part of monitoring to detect any new cancer growth. These surveillance intervals help ensure early detection of recurrent or new disease, improving long-term outcomes.
Relationships:
  - Source: COLONOSCOPY
  - Target: SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Colonoscopy is a surveillance test recommended at scheduled intervals after colon cancer treatment.

---</data>
      <data key="d2">1</data>
