    <node id="RISK OF DISEASE PROGRESSION: THE FUNDAMENTAL RISK IS THAT THE CANCER MAY PROGRESS DURING THE SURVEILLANCE PERIOD. THIS IS WHY REGULAR TESTING, REPEAT BIOPSIES, AND ADVANCED IMAGING ARE INCORPORATED INTO THE PROTOCOL—TO DETECT ANY SIGNIFICANT PROGRESSION PROMPTLY [DATA: ENTITIES (406)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4" />
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPSY-RELATED COMPLICATIONS: SINCE ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE COMMONLY INVOLVES MORE THAN ONE BIOPSY, PATIENTS MAY EXPERIENCE COMPLICATIONS FROM THESE PROCEDURES. DOCUMENTED COMPLICATIONS MAY INCLUDE INFECTION, BLEEDING FROM THE RECTUM, OR BLOOD IN THE URINE, STOOL, OR SEMEN. THESE USUALLY RESOLVE AFTER A FEW DAYS OR, WITH SEMEN, AFTER A FEW WEEKS [DATA: SOURCES (126)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4" />
    </node>
    <node id="NEED FOR CONSISTENT ADHERENCE: ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE REQUIRES CONSISTENT ADHERENCE TO FOLLOW-UP SCHEDULES. IF PATIENTS DO NOT ADHERE TO THE MONITORING PROTOCOL, THERE IS A RISK THAT CANCER PROGRESSION COULD GO UNDETECTED [DATA: ENTITIES (406)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4" />
    </node>
    <node id="TREATMENT DELAY: TREATMENT IS DELIBERATELY DELAYED, AND INTERVENTION IS INITIATED ONLY IF THE CANCER CHANGES ENOUGH TO REQUIRE IT. THIS MEANS THAT IF THE CANCER DOES PROGRESS, TREATMENT MAY BE DELAYED COMPARED TO IMMEDIATE INTERVENTION [DATA: ENTITIES (406)].">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4" />
    </node>
    <node id="RISK OF DISEASE PROGRESSION:">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4"> The fundamental risk is that the cancer may progress during the surveillance period. This is why regular testing, repeat biopsies, and advanced imaging are incorporated into the protocol—to detect any significant progression promptly [Data: Entities (406)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOPSY-RELATED COMPLICATIONS:">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4"> Since active surveillance commonly involves more than one biopsy, patients may experience complications from these procedures. Documented complications may include infection, bleeding from the rectum, or blood in the urine, stool, or semen. These usually resolve after a few days or, with semen, after a few weeks [Data: Sources (126)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEED FOR CONSISTENT ADHERENCE:">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4"> Active surveillance requires consistent adherence to follow-up schedules. If patients do not adhere to the monitoring protocol, there is a risk that cancer progression could go undetected [Data: Entities (406)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TREATMENT DELAY:">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1" />
      <data key="d2">1</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4"> Treatment is deliberately delayed, and intervention is initiated only if the cancer changes enough to require it. This means that if the cancer does progress, treatment may be delayed compared to immediate intervention [Data: Entities (406)].
</data>
    </node>
