ENTITY: APL
Description: APL (acute promyelocytic leukemia) is a rare subtype of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). It is characterized by the abnormal fusion gene PML::RARA, which results from a translocation between chromosomes 15 and 17. This genetic abnormality is a defining feature of the disease and drives its clinical behavior. APL is discussed in Chapter 5 of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which provides information about this specific leukemia subtype. As a distinct form of AML, APL requires specialized diagnostic evaluation and management approaches due to its unique genetic basis and clinical characteristics.
Relationships: (None listed in the data tables)

ENTITY: GENETIC AND BIOMARKER TESTS
Description: Genetic and biomarker tests are laboratory tests used in the management of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). These tests are performed to learn about the specific subtype of AML a patient has, to identify targeted treatment options, and to help determine prognosis. By analyzing genetic and biomarker information, healthcare providers can better understand the characteristics of the disease and tailor therapeutic strategies accordingly.
Relationships: (None listed in the data tables)

ENTITY: MUTATION
Description: Mutation is a change or error in the genetic code of a cell, specifically an abnormal alteration in genes or chromosomes. These changes can be inherited from one’s parents or acquired during a person’s lifetime, and they may increase the risk of developing cancer. Mutations are the underlying starting point for many cancers, including bladder cancer and acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where they are often unique to the cancer cells themselves. In the context of AML, mutation testing is performed using techniques such as karyotype analysis, fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH), next-generation sequencing (NGS), and polymerase chain reaction (PCR). These tests identify specific genetic abnormalities that can guide treatment decisions. Importantly, some mutations can be directly targeted with specific therapies, making mutation profiling a critical component of personalized cancer care. Overall, a mutation represents a fundamental error or change in the genetic material that can drive cancer development and, when identified, offers opportunities for tailored treatment strategies.
Relationships: (None listed in the data tables)

ENTITY: DNA SEQUENCING
Description: A biomarker test to look for mutations in TP53 and IGHV genes in CLL cells.
Relationships: (None listed in the data tables)

ENTITY: FISH
Description: Fluorescence in situ hybridization; a biomarker test to detect chromosome abnormalities such as 17p deletion in CLL cells.
Relationships: (None listed in the data tables)
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEMATOPOIETIC CELL TRANSPLANT">
      <data key="d0">extracted</data>
      <data key="d1">HEMATOPOIETIC CELL TRANSPLANT is a medical treatment that replaces the patient's blood-forming stem cells. It is used as a therapeutic approach for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), serving as a treatment type specifically aimed at restoring healthy blood cell production by substituting the patient's compromised stem cells with new, healthy ones. This concise definition encompasses the procedure's core function as a stem cell replacement therapy and its application in the context of AML treatment.
Relationships: (None explicitly listed in the provided data tables)

---</data>
