ENTITY: CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA
Description: Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is the disease process discussed in the text; it is caused by the BCR::ABL1 gene, characterized by the Philadelphia chromosome, and the text covers testing, treatment, and care for it.
Relationships:
  - Source: CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA
  - Target: BCR::ABL1
  - Description: BCR::ABL1 produces a protein that leads to uncontrolled blood cell growth, ultimately causing CML.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA
  - Description: The NCCN Guidelines for Patients are referenced as a resource to help patients with cancer, including CML, understand their care.
  - Source: CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA
  - Target: PHILADELPHIA CHROMOSOME
  - Description: The Philadelphia chromosome is the hallmark of CML; if it is absent, CML is not present.
  - Source: CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA
  - Target: BLAST PHASE CML
  - Description: Blast phase CML is an advanced form of CML, and the type of blast cells in BP-CML affects treatment.

ENTITY: RET MUTATIONS
Description: RET gene mutations tested as biomarkers in recurrent ovarian cancer.
Relationships:
  - Source: RET MUTATIONS
  - Target: BIOMARKER TESTING
  - Description: RET mutations are tested as biomarkers in recurrent ovarian cancer.

ENTITY: BCR::ABL1
Description: BCR::ABL1 is an abnormal fusion gene created by the same chromosomal translocation that produces the Philadelphia chromosome; it encodes a protein that leads to uncontrolled blood cell growth and ultimately CML.
Relationships:
  - Source: CHRONIC MYELOID LEUKEMIA
  - Target: BCR::ABL1
  - Description: BCR::ABL1 produces a protein that leads to uncontrolled blood cell growth, ultimately causing CML.
  - Source: PHILADELPHIA CHROMOSOME
  - Target: BCR::ABL1
  - Description: Both are formed from the same translocation between chromosome 9 and chromosome 22: BCR::ABL1 is the fused gene, and the Philadelphia chromosome is the resulting abnormal chromosome 22.

ENTITY: CLL SPECIALIST
Description: The CLL specialist is a doctor who specializes in chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) and provided a consultation. During this consultation, the CLL specialist was reassuring, answered all questions thoroughly, and gave advice about managing CLL.
Relationships:
