    <edge source="NCCN" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) and the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) are both recognized resources for locating clinical trials. NCCN provides clinical trial resources specifically for the United States, primarily through its network of NCCN Cancer Centers. In contrast, the U.S. National Library of Medicine offers worldwide clinical trial resources, most notably through the ClinicalTrials.gov database. Together, they serve as complementary tools: NCCN focuses on U.S.-based trial options, while NLM covers global trial listings.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE (NLM)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients list the U.S. National Library of Medicine as a resource.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE (NLM)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="METASTATIC BREAST CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN provides comprehensive resources for patients and caregivers about metastatic breast cancer. Specifically, NCCN publishes the patient guideline titled "Breast Cancer Metastatic," which offers detailed information on this condition. This guideline is accessible through NCCN's official patient resources, including the NCCN.org/patientguidelines website and the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer mobile app. These materials are designed to help individuals understand metastatic breast cancer, its management, and related care considerations. NCCN thus serves as a key source of patient-focused education and support for those affected by metastatic breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
