    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">External beam radiation therapy is a type of radiation therapy. It is delivered from outside the body using a linear accelerator, with a large machine used to aim radiation at the cancer site. External beam radiation therapy is described as the usual type of radiation therapy for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supportive care is used to relieve side effects caused by radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy and surgery are two distinct treatment modalities that are often used in combination for cancer care. Surgery is used to physically remove tumors, while radiation therapy uses high-energy radiation to destroy cancer cells. In many cases, treatment plans integrate both approaches: after surgery, radiation therapy is commonly administered to decrease the chance of the tumor returning and to kill any remaining cancer cells that were not removed during the operation. Additionally, radiation therapy serves as an alternative treatment option for patients who cannot undergo surgery or who choose not to have it. Thus, radiation therapy can be employed either as a standalone treatment for those ineligible for or declining surgery, or as an adjuvant therapy following surgery to improve local control and reduce the risk of recurrence. Together, surgery and radiation therapy form a complementary strategy in oncology, with the specific sequence and combination tailored to each patient's situation.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is typically given Monday through Friday for 5 to 6 weeks as part of chemoradiation for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy uses high-energy x-rays to kill cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy may be used in addition to chemotherapy for stage IV anal cancer that has not had prior radiation.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">RADIATION THERAPY is a medical treatment that uses high-energy radiation to damage or destroy cancer cells. It is a core component in oncology and may be used alone or in combination with other treatments. CHEMORADIATION is a treatment approach that combines radiation therapy with chemotherapy. It is defined as the simultaneous or coordinated use of these two modalities, given together to enhance the overall therapeutic effect. Chemoradiation includes radiation therapy as one of its essential components, alongside systemic chemotherapy. This combination is used to improve treatment outcomes by leveraging the radiosensitizing properties of chemotherapy, which can make cancer cells more vulnerable to radiation, while also addressing cancer cells that may have spread beyond the primary site. Thus, chemoradiation represents an integrated strategy in cancer care, bringing together the localized effects of radiation therapy with the systemic effects of chemotherapy in a single, combined treatment regimen.</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIATION THERAPY" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a treatment used for primary CNS lymphoma, a cancer that affects the central nervous system. It is considered a main treatment option for this condition, playing a key role in managing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
