    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging tests and scans are used to find prostate cancer and determine whether it has spread</data>
      <data key="d8">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PATIENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The patient should obtain copies of imaging results and keep them organized.</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging is used after cervical cancer treatment to detect recurrence or spread.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PSA TEST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A PSA test is often paired with imaging to get a more complete picture of prostate health</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">IMAGING and BIOPSY are complementary procedures in the management of glioma and other cancers, though the order in which they are used depends on the clinical context.

In cases where a biopsy has already confirmed disease progression, IMAGING is the next step to determine whether the cancer is locally progressive or has become metastatic. This helps guide further treatment by clarifying the extent and spread of the disease.

Conversely, when initial IMAGING findings suggest the presence of a glioma, a BIOPSY is then performed as the next step to confirm the diagnosis. In this scenario, imaging serves as the screening or detection tool that prompts tissue sampling for pathological confirmation.

Thus, the relationship between IMAGING and BIOPSY is bidirectional: imaging can lead to biopsy when a suspicious lesion is found, and biopsy can lead to imaging when progression is confirmed, each serving distinct roles in diagnosis and disease monitoring. Together, they form an integrated diagnostic and surveillance pathway for brain tumors and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), also referred to simply as IMAGING in certain contexts, is a type of imaging that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed pictures of the body's internal structures. As an imaging test, MRI provides highly detailed images without the use of ionizing radiation, making it a valuable diagnostic tool.</data>
      <data key="d8">MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="MRI" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) is a type of IMAGING test used to create detailed pictures of the inside of the body. As an imaging modality, MRI serves as an initial diagnostic tool and is frequently employed in oncology, particularly for cervical cancer. Specifically, MRI is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging, helping clinicians assess the extent of disease at diagnosis. It is also one of the imaging tests used in surveillance after recurrence treatment, allowing for monitoring of patients following therapy for recurrent disease. Additionally, MRI with contrast is utilized as an imaging method for pelvic follow-up of cervical cancer, with particular importance after fertility-sparing treatment. Thus, MRI functions across multiple stages of cervical cancer care: initial evaluation/staging, post-treatment surveillance, and long-term pelvic follow-up, depending on the clinical context and whether contrast enhancement is used. As a versatile IMAGING technique, MRI provides essential anatomical and pathological information that guides diagnosis, treatment planning, and monitoring in cervical cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging, specifically computed tomography (CT), serves as an initial imaging modality and is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging. Additionally, CT with contrast is an imaging option for follow-up of cervical cancer stages 2, 3, and 4A.</data>
      <data key="d8">CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging tests are used to confirm primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PHYSICAL EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A physical exam that is suspicious for recurrence leads to imaging tests to confirm whether cancer has returned.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICAL EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CT OR MRI OF ABDOMEN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT or MRI of the abdomen is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d8">CT OR MRI OF ABDOMEN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Diagnostic mammogram is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="BREAST MRI" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Breast MRI is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CT SCAN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging encompasses various diagnostic techniques, including the CT scan (computed tomography scan). A CT scan is an imaging test that combines multiple x-ray images to create a detailed three-dimensional picture of internal structures. In clinical practice, CT is one of the imaging tests used in surveillance after recurrence treatment, allowing healthcare providers to monitor patients for signs of recurrence and assess response to therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team uses imaging tests to locate and assess a possible brain tumor.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="RECURRENCE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging is used to monitor for recurrence or response to therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="DIAGNOSTIC TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging is a type of diagnostic test.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging refers to the use of diagnostic tests and scans, which are also called imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients are told to ask their treatment team whether they meet the requirements for a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The clinical trials process is closely coordinated with the patients care team. Patients are advised to ask their care team about access to clinical trials, and the care team plays a central role in facilitating clinical trial participation. In this capacity, the care team also advises the patient on whether a clinical trial might be right for them, helping to guide the decision-making process. Together, these responsibilities ensure that patients receive personalized support and clear information when considering clinical trial options.</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN Cancer Centers are listed as a resource for finding clinical trials in the United States</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCI provides clinical trial search information at cancer.gov and supports the Cancer Information Service</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN provides a clinical trial finding resource at NCCN.org/cancercenters.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials are presented as a possible treatment option for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NLM operates clinicaltrials.gov, a database for finding clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CIS helps people find clinical trials and answers questions about them</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="RESEARCH TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials are research studies conducted to evaluate medical, surgical, or behavioral interventions. These trials are managed by a dedicated research team, which plays a central role in the study process. The research team is responsible for reviewing the study details with participants, ensuring they understand the purpose, procedures, risks, and potential benefits before enrollment. In addition to guiding participants through the informed consent process, the research team actively helps collect research data throughout the trial. They also coordinate patient care, working alongside healthcare providers to ensure that participants receive appropriate medical attention and that the trial protocol is followed. Thus, the research team bridges the gap between clinical care and research, supporting both data integrity and participant well-being. Together, clinical trials and the research team form an integrated framework for advancing medical knowledge while maintaining patient safety and care coordination.</data>
      <data key="d8">RESEARCH TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="STUDY SPONSOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The study sponsor pays for research-related costs, including the study drug, so there is no fee to enroll.</data>
      <data key="d8">STUDY SPONSOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIALS" target="FDA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials give people access to investigational treatments that may, in time, be approved by the FDA. FDA may approve a treatment after Phase 3 clinical trial results are good, and Phase 4 trials study safety of FDA-approved treatments.</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The treatment team considers many factors to decide if a stem cell rescue is right for a patient</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Supportive care helps manage side effects and symptoms that arise from stem cell rescue and its high-dose chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stem cell rescue may be used for primary CNS lymphoma after other treatments or relapse</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stem cell rescue is used when lymphoma comes back after earlier treatment or after other treatments</data>
      <data key="d8">LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STEM CELL RESCUE" target="RELAPSE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stem cell rescue is a treatment approach used for lymphoma when the disease has returned after an initial course of therapy. Specifically, it is indicated in the context of a relapse, meaning the lymphoma has come back following earlier treatment. The procedure is employed after the relapse occurs, serving as a subsequent line of intervention. Thus, stem cell rescue is directly associated with managing recurrent lymphoma, with the goal of addressing the disease after prior treatment has failed to provide a lasting remission.</data>
      <data key="d8">RELAPSE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="DIAGNOSTIC TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scans are diagnostic imaging tests</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scan obtains pictures of the cancer sites</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="MRI" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scans and MRIs are both imaging procedures used to create detailed pictures of areas inside the body, and both may involve the use of contrast dye to enhance the images. They are often considered alternative imaging options for surveillance, particularly in oncology. For example, either a CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis, or a CT scan of the chest combined with an MRI of the abdomen and pelvis, may be used depending on the clinical situation. Both modalities are commonly used to determine how far bladder cancer has spread, helping doctors assess the extent of disease. The experience of getting an MRI scan is similar to that of getting a CT scan, but the MRI generally takes longer to complete. Together, CT and MRI provide versatile and complementary tools for diagnosing and monitoring a range of medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="MRI SCAN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scans and MRI scans are both medical imaging tests used to visualize internal structures of the body, but they differ in several key aspects. MRI scans take longer to perform than CT scans, making the overall duration of an MRI exam generally more time-consuming. Additionally, the types of contrast material used for CT and MRI are different; CT scans typically use iodine-based contrast agents, whereas MRI scans commonly use gadolinium-based contrast agents. These differences in contrast materials reflect the distinct imaging technologies and the specific diagnostic information each scan provides.</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scan is an imaging test used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="UROGRAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A urogram can be performed using CT to evaluate the urinary tract.</data>
      <data key="d8">UROGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CT scan is an imaging procedure.</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) can play a key role in biopsy procedures. It is capable of revealing suspicious areas within the body that may require sampling by biopsy. Additionally, MRI can be used to guide a biopsy, helping to ensure that the procedure targets the identified area accurately. Thus, MRI serves both as a detection tool and as a guidance method for biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If surgery is needed or being considered, MRI is ordered to evaluate the depth and spread of rectal cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team relies on MRI scans as a key diagnostic and monitoring tool throughout the patients journey, including during diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up care. Because the MRI procedure involves being inside an enclosed scanner, the care team should be informed if the patient feels nervous or anxious in enclosed spaces before the scan takes place. This allows the team to make appropriate accommodations and ensure the patients comfort and safety.</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CT SCAN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI and CT scan are both medical imaging modalities used to visualize internal structures for diagnostic purposes. They are similar in that each may involve the use of a contrast agent to enhance image quality, but the contrast materials used for MRI and CT scan are different. An MRI scan, while similar to a CT scan, generally takes longer and uses a more enclosed machine, which can feel more confining to patients. CT scans are often employed as an alternative when MRI is unavailable or is not recommended for a particular patient. Conversely, MRI may be used to investigate cancer in the abdomen, especially in the liver, when a patient cannot receive the contrast agent typically used for a CT scan. In such cases, MRI offers a viable diagnostic option with a contrast agent that the patient can tolerate. Overall, the two tests complement each other: CT scans provide a quicker and more accessible imaging method, while MRI offers detailed imaging with a different contrast agent profile, making it useful when CT contrast is contraindicated or when MRI is otherwise preferable.</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">MRI is an imaging test used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="PET SCAN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">PET scans may be combined with MRI for imaging.</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
    </edge>
