    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="MUTATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing is done to look for inherited and tumor-related mutations</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="HEREDITARY GENE TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hereditary gene testing is one of the two basic types of genetic testing for prostate cancer care</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with ovarian cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In tumor testing, a type of genetic testing, a biopsy sample is used for analysis</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing may be done through the gynecology or oncology care team</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="DOCTOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Doctors use health history, family history, and genetic testing to assess hereditary ovarian cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="GERMLINE TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing is a broad term that encompasses various methods used to analyze an individual's DNA. Germline testing is a specific type of genetic testing that focuses on identifying inherited mutations. These mutations are present from birth and can be passed down from parents to their children. By looking for these inherited changes, germline testing helps assess an individual's risk for certain genetic conditions and can inform medical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="GENETIC COUNSELOR" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Genetic testing may be done through a genetic counselor, who helps patients understand the results</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENETIC TESTING" target="MAINTENANCE THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">BRCA status determined by genetic testing guides decisions about maintenance therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEPATITIS B AND HEPATITIS C SCREENING" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hepatitis blood test tells the treatment team whether the patient has or had hepatitis B or C.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEPATITIS B AND HEPATITIS C SCREENING" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Screening is needed because some cancer treatments can reactivate hepatitis B or hepatitis C and cause liver damage.</data>
      <data key="d8">HEPATITIS B AND HEPATITIS C SCREENING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER DIAGNOSIS" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">After a cancer diagnosis, the treatment team screens distress as part of the patient's cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FAMILY HISTORY" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team is responsible for collecting and reviewing information related to family history as part of patient assessments. Specifically, the care team asks about family history, gathering details about the patient's relatives and their health conditions. This process is also described as asking about the family medical history, meaning the care team solicits information regarding any medical conditions, diseases, or health patterns that may run in the patient's family. In practice, the care team's inquiry into family history encompasses the broader family medical history, ensuring that relevant hereditary and genetic factors are considered in the patient's overall care plan.</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FAMILY HISTORY" target="GENETIC TESTING" id="0">
