    <edge source="TOBACCO AND ALCOHOL USE HISTORY" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The care team asks about tobacco and alcohol use history</data>
      <data key="d8">TOBACCO AND ALCOHOL USE HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENERAL HEALTH TESTS" target="TOBACCO AND ALCOHOL USE HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">General health tests include tobacco and alcohol use history</data>
      <data key="d8">TOBACCO AND ALCOHOL USE HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENERAL HEALTH TESTS" target="FAMILY HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">General health tests include family history</data>
      <data key="d8">GENERAL HEALTH TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENERAL HEALTH TESTS" target="MEDICAL HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">General health tests encompass a range of evaluations used to assess an individual's overall well-being, and they include the medical history as a fundamental component. Specifically, the medical history is recognized as one of the key general health tests, meaning it is an integral part of the initial health assessment process. Beyond simply being a component, the medical history plays a crucial role in guiding clinical decision-making: it helps determine which additional tests and treatments are relevant and appropriate for the individual. In this way, the medical history both serves as a foundational element of general health testing and shapes the subsequent course of care based on the patient's unique background, prior conditions, and risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENERAL HEALTH TESTS" target="PHYSICAL EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">GENERAL HEALTH TESTS encompass a range of routine medical evaluations, and PHYSICAL EXAM is one of the key components included within them. In other words, a physical exam is one of the general health tests performed to assess overall health.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICAL EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENERAL HEALTH TESTS" target="LIVER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">General health tests are part of diagnosing liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GENERAL HEALTH TESTS" target="TESTING FOR NPC" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">(No description provided in the data tables)</data>
      <data key="d8">GENERAL HEALTH TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="FAMILY HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Some cancers can run in families, so family history is discussed.</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="MUTATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer begins with mutations in genes. These genetic mutations can cause cancer by disrupting normal cell regulation. Tumor tests can identify specific mutations, and those mutations can then be targeted with specific therapies.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="TUMOR MUTATION TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tumor mutation testing examines cancer cells for DNA mutations that may be targeted with specific therapies.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="BLADDER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bladder cancer is a type of cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Imaging tests can show whether cancer has spread</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="STAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Staging describes how much cancer is in the body, how far it has grown, and where it has spread</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="GERMLINE TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Germline testing may be appropriate for people who have had cancer or high grade cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="SMOKING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Smoking increases the risk of developing cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="QUITTING SMOKING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The stress of having cancer can make it harder to quit smoking. Quitting smoking may reduce the risk of getting cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="GENETIC COUNSELING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">People who have already had cancer are among those for whom genetic counseling may be appropriate</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER" target="HEAD AND NECK CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Head and neck cancer is a type of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
    </edge>
