    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="FAMILY HISTORY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Medical history and family history are two related but distinct health histories used by the care team. Family history is a specific component of the broader medical history. While the medical history encompasses a patients overall health background, the family history focuses specifically on the health conditions and patterns that occur among blood relatives. Both are important for guiding clinical care, but family history contributes to the medical history by providing insight into inherited risks and familial health trends.</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="CARE TEAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The medical history is a record of a patients symptoms, medicines, and related health information, which is shared with the care team during consultations. The care team relies on this medical history to help determine the best treatment plan for the patient. In turn, the patient provides details about symptoms and current medications during the medical history process, enabling the care team to make informed decisions. Together, the medical history and the care team form a collaborative loop: the patient supplies essential information, and the care team uses it to guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Doctors ask about surgeries during medical history.</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
