    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="HIV" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A weakened immune system due to HIV is a risk factor for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">HIV</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="AIDS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A weakened immune system due to AIDS is a risk factor for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">AIDS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Chemotherapy is a treatment option for cervical cancer, and it is also used as part of a broader treatment approach for this condition. In some cases, chemotherapy may be the primary treatment, while in others it is combined with other therapies such as surgery or radiation. Thus, chemotherapy plays a role both as a standalone option and as a component of a multidisciplinary treatment plan for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="CERVICAL INTRAEPITHELIAL NEOPLASIA (CIN)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer starts as CIN, areas of abnormal pre-cancerous cells on the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL INTRAEPITHELIAL NEOPLASIA (CIN)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="STAGE 3C CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stage 3C cervical cancer is a stage of cervical cancer with cancer in pelvic and/or para-aortic lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d8">STAGE 3C CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="STAGE 3B CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stage 3B cervical cancer is a stage of cervical cancer that has grown into the pelvic wall and/or caused kidney dysfunction.</data>
      <data key="d8">STAGE 3B CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="STAGE 3A CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stage 3A cervical cancer is a stage of cervical cancer that has grown into the lower third of the vagina.</data>
      <data key="d8">STAGE 3A CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="STAGE 1B3 CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stage 1B3 cervical cancer is a stage of cervical cancer confined to the cervix and larger than 4 cm.</data>
      <data key="d8">STAGE 1B3 CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="STAGE 2A CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Stage 2A is a stage of cervical cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">STAGE 2A CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="HPV VACCINATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">By preventing HPV infection, HPV vaccination can reduce the risk of HPV-related cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Surgery is a treatment option for cervical cancer and is particularly effective in the early stages of the disease. In fact, surgery is often the main treatment for early-stage cervical cancer, offering a primary approach to removing the cancerous tissue. Together, these statements underscore that surgical intervention plays a key role in managing cervical cancer, especially when the cancer has not spread extensively. The use of surgery is thus most commonly recommended for patients diagnosed at an early stage, where it can serve as a definitive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Treatment is given for cervical cancer, and options depend on the extent of the cancer and other factors.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radiation therapy is a treatment option and a type of treatment for cervical cancer. It is used as a therapeutic approach in the management of this disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer is a specific type of cancer that originates in the cervix, which is the lower part of the uterus connecting to the vagina. As a distinct form of the broader disease category known as cancer, cervical cancer is characterized by the uncontrolled growth and division of abnormal cells in the cervical tissue. Being classified as a type of cancer means that it shares the fundamental features of cancer, such as the potential to invade nearby tissues and spread to other parts of the body if not detected and treated early. Cervical cancer is most commonly caused by persistent infection with high-risk strains of the human papillomavirus (HPV), though this detail is not explicitly mentioned in the provided descriptions, it is a well-known aspect of the condition. The descriptions emphasize that cervical cancer is a specific cancer type, occurring in the cervix, and is not a general term but a precise diagnosis within oncology. Thus, cervical cancer falls under the overarching classification of cancer, but it is distinguished by its anatomical site of origin and its unique biological behavior, risk factors, and prevention strategies. In summary, cervical cancer is a type of cancer that arises in the cervix, making it one of the many site-specific cancers within the broader category of malignant diseases. This concise definition captures the essential relationship between the general disease entity, cancer, and this particular manifestation, cervical cancer. The information from all descriptions is consistent and non-contradictory, reinforcing that cervical cancer is both a type of cancer and specifically localized to the cervix. Therefore, the comprehensive description of cervical cancer is that it is a malignant neoplasm of the cervix, classified under the umbrella term cancer, with all the implications that such a classification carries for diagnosis, treatment, and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Imaging plays a critical role in the clinical management of cervical cancer, particularly in the pre-treatment phase. Before any therapeutic intervention is initiated, imaging is used to determine the extent of the cancera process known as staging. By evaluating how far the cancer has spread within the cervix, into nearby tissues, or to distant organs, imaging provides essential information that guides treatment planning. This pre-treatment assessment helps clinicians choose the most appropriate therapeutic approach, whether surgery, radiation, chemotherapy, or a combination of these modalities. Accurate staging via imaging is therefore fundamental to optimizing outcomes for patients diagnosed with cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Metastasis is the spread of cervical cancer to distant sites, corresponding to stage 4B.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="BLOOD TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Blood tests are used before treatment to assess general health and organ function in people with cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Listed as a type of treatment for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Listed as a type of treatment for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CERVICAL CANCER and CLINICAL TRIALS are directly connected in the context of treatment options. Clinical trials are recognized as a legitimate treatment option for cervical cancer, specifically serving as a pathway for studying investigational treatments. They are formally listed among the treatment options available to patients diagnosed with cervical cancer, offering access to experimental therapies that are not yet part of standard care. In summary, clinical trials represent a distinct and listed treatment approach for cervical cancer, focused on evaluating new and potentially effective interventions.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Among the various treatment options available for this condition, external beam radiation therapy is specifically listed as a type of treatment for cervical cancer. External beam radiation therapy uses high-energy X-rays or other types of radiation to destroy cancer cells or keep them from growing. It is delivered from a machine outside the body, targeting the pelvic area where the cervical tumor is located. This therapy is often used as part of a comprehensive treatment plan for cervical cancer, either alone or in combination with other modalities such as chemotherapy or brachytherapy (internal radiation). The use of external beam radiation therapy for cervical cancer is well established, and it may be employed at different stages of the disease, including as an alternative to surgery for certain patients, or postoperatively to eliminate any remaining cancer cells. Thus, the relationship between cervical cancer and external beam radiation therapy is that the latter is a recognized and commonly applied treatment option for the former.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="PREGNANCY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer treatment may make natural pregnancy impossible, leading to fertility preservation discussions.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="CYSTOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cystoscopy is a medical procedure used to visually examine the interior of the bladder and urinary tract. In the context of cervical cancer, cystoscopy plays an important diagnostic role. Specifically, cystoscopy is used to look for signs of cancer within the bladder, and it may be needed to detect whether cervical cancer has spread to the bladder. Additionally, cystoscopy may be used to check both the bladder and the urinary tract for evidence of cervical cancer metastasis. Thus, for individuals with cervical cancer, cystoscopy helps determine whether the cancer has invaded nearby urinary structures, aiding in staging and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="BRACHYTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Listed as a type of treatment for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="CONE BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure that removes a cone-shaped piece of tissue from the cervix. It serves a dual purpose in the management of cervical cancer: it is used both as a diagnostic tool to evaluate the extent of cancer and as a treatment option. Specifically, cone biopsy is recommended for some small, early-stage cervical cancers, where it can be used to remove the cancerous tissue entirely. Additionally, cone biopsy may be performed to gather more information after an initial cervical biopsy, helping to confirm the diagnosis or determine how far the cancer has spread. In summary, for select cases of early cervical cancer, cone biopsy is a valuable and recommended approach for both assessment and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="STAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer staging is a critical process that determines the extent of the disease and directly informs treatment decisions. Staging is a rating of how far the cancer has spread, and for cervical cancer, the universally used system is the FIGO (International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics) system. By assessing the tumor's size, location, and spread to nearby tissues or distant organs, healthcare providers can classify the cancer into specific stages, which guides prognosis and therapeutic strategy. Thus, staging serves as the foundation for managing cervical cancer, ensuring that treatment is tailored to the individual patient's condition.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Testing helps determine the extent of cancer before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="PELVIC EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A Pap test performed during a pelvic exam can detect cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">PELVIC EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="HYSTERECTOMY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hysterectomy is surgery to remove the uterus and cervix to treat cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="EXAMINATIONS UNDER ANESTHESIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Examinations under anesthesia may be needed to check nearby organs for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">EXAMINATIONS UNDER ANESTHESIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="PARA-AORTIC NODES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer can spread to para-aortic lymph nodes in the abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d8">PARA-AORTIC NODES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="PROCTOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Proctoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the anus and rectum, and it plays a role in evaluating certain cancers. Specifically, proctoscopy is used to look for signs of cancer in the bowel. In the context of cervical cancer, proctoscopy may be needed to look for signs of cervical cancer in the bowel. Additionally, proctoscopy may be used to check the anus and rectum for cervical cancer spread, helping to determine whether the cancer has metastasized to these areas. Thus, for cervical cancer patients, proctoscopy serves as a diagnostic tool to assess possible bowel involvement and local spread of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">PROCTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HPV" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
