    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN is the abbreviated name of the National Comprehensive Cancer Network</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="NCCN FOUNDATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The NCCN Foundation funds and distributes the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which are developed, updated, and hosted by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network. The NCCN Foundation funds and distributes the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which are developed, updated, and hosted independently by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network is an alliance of leading cancer centers across the United States.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) publishes patient care guidelines specifically for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL). These NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide comprehensive information about ALL, including details on testing, treatment options, and supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="PFIZER INC." id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pfizer Inc. supports the availability of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, while the National Comprehensive Cancer Network independently adapts, updates, and hosts those guidelines. Pfizer Inc. supports the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which are independently adapted, updated, and hosted by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical guidance that includes a dedicated chapter on the treatment of BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL. This information is presented in the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which specifically address management and treatment options for this subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines that include a chapter on BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL, which is identified as the most common type of B-ALL. The NCCN Guidelines for Patients also contain a chapter specifically addressing treatment for BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL, offering patient-focused information on managing this condition.</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical practice guidelines that address the treatment of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines include a dedicated chapter on B-ALL treatment, and the NCCN Guidelines for Patients also contain a chapter covering this topic. These resources are designed to help healthcare professionals and patients understand evidence-based approaches to managing B-ALL, from diagnosis through treatment planning. The NCCN thus offers comprehensive, standardized guidance on B-ALL, ensuring that both clinical practitioners and patients have access to current, expert-reviewed information on therapeutic options for this disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="T-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a professional organization that develops evidence-based guidelines for cancer care. Its NCCN Guidelines include a chapter dedicated to T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL). This chapter appears in both the patient-focused version of the guidelines, where it discusses T-ALL in an accessible manner, and in the clinical guidelines, where it provides detailed treatment recommendations. Thus, the NCCN Guidelines serve as a resource for understanding T-ALL and its management, covering both educational and therapeutic aspects of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d8">T-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="BPDCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a professional organization that develops clinical practice guidelines for cancer care. Among its resources, the NCCN Guidelines for Patients include a dedicated chapter addressing the treatment of BPDCNblastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm, a rare and aggressive blood cancer. This chapter is part of the NCCN's patient-focused educational materials, which are designed to help patients understand their diagnosis and treatment options. The NCCN's coverage of BPDCN in its patient guidelines reflects the organization's commitment to providing up-to-date, evidence-based information for both clinicians and patients. The inclusion of this chapter ensures that individuals affected by BPDCN have accessible guidance on available therapeutic approaches, as outlined by the NCCN's expert panel. Thus, the NCCN directly supports patient education and informed decision-making for BPDCN through this specific chapter in its guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d8">BPDCN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="NON-HODGKIN LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The NCCN Guidelines mention non-Hodgkin lymphoma when explaining Burkitt lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d8">NON-HODGKIN LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The NCCN Guidelines mention mature B-cell ALL/Burkitt leukemia as a rare subtype of B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA TEST" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The PSA test is a common test used to detect and monitor prostate cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA TEST" target="DIAGNOSTIC TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
