      <data key="d7">If a PSA test suggests prostate cancer, diagnostic testing may be offered.</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA TEST" target="BIOMARKER TEST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biomarker tests are used in addition to PSA to help decide whether further testing is needed.</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA TEST" target="PSA LEVEL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A PSA test measures the amount of PSA in the bloodstream</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA TEST" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The PSA test and prostate biopsy are closely related procedures in the evaluation of prostate health. The PSA test measures the level of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, and its results help doctors decide whether a prostate biopsy is needed. If PSA levels are elevated or show concerning changes, a biopsy may be recommended to check for prostate cancer or other conditions. However, it is important to note that the PSA level can rise temporarily after a prostate biopsy, which can affect interpretation of subsequent PSA measurements. Thus, while the PSA test serves as a screening and decision-making tool, the biopsy itself can transiently influence PSA levels, and this should be considered in follow-up assessments. Together, these two procedures form a sequence: PSA testing guides the need for biopsy, and biopsy results provide a definitive diagnosis, while the temporary PSA elevation after biopsy is a known clinical phenomenon.</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA TEST" target="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The PSA test (prostate-specific antigen test) and digital rectal exam are two initial assessments commonly used to evaluate the health of the prostate gland, particularly for the early detection or suspicion of prostate cancer. Both procedures are often performed together as part of a routine screening or when symptoms suggest a possible prostate issue. The results from the PSA test and digital rectal exam provide complementary information: the PSA test measures the level of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, which can be elevated in the presence of prostate cancer, while the digital rectal exam allows a clinician to manually feel the prostate for any abnormal lumps, hardness, or irregularities in size and shape. Together, these two assessments are frequently paired to help determine whether further testing, such as a prostate biopsy, is needed. While neither test alone can definitively diagnose prostate cancer, their combined findings are a crucial step in identifying men who may require more invasive diagnostic procedures. In summary, the PSA test and digital rectal exam are essential initial screening tools that work together to assess prostate health and guide decisions about the need for biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSA TEST" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A rise in PSA after treatment may indicate that treatment is losing effectiveness</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA TEST</data>
    </edge>
