    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. Specifically, chemoradiation includes chemotherapy administered concurrently with radiation therapy, meaning the drug component is given at the same time as the radiation treatment. In the context of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy serves as the drug component delivered concurrently with EBRT as part of the chemoradiation regimen. The available descriptions indicate that chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both may be listed as possible next treatment options for a patient, depending on the clinical scenario. Chemotherapy, as the drug component, works in conjunction with radiation to enhance the overall treatment effect. Thus, chemoradiation represents the integration of systemic drug therapy and localized radiation, while chemotherapy itself can also be considered independently as a treatment option.</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMORADIATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is a standard treatment approach for cervical cancer, particularly in cases where the disease is locally advanced. It is strongly recommended for most locally advanced cervical cancers, reflecting its role as a primary therapeutic strategy when the tumor has spread beyond the early stages within the cervix. Additionally, chemoradiation is indicated when cancer is found in lymph nodes or in tissues outside the cervix, meaning it is also used when there is regional or locoregional spread beyond the primary site. In both scenarioslocally advanced disease and involvement of lymph nodes or extra-cervical tissueschemoradiation combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy to enhance tumor control and improve overall outcomes. This dual-modality treatment leverages the radiosensitizing properties of chemotherapy to make radiation more effective against cancer cells. The recommendations underscore that chemoradiation is a cornerstone of management for cervical cancer cases that are not amenable to surgery alone or that carry a higher risk of recurrence due to advanced local extension or lymphatic spread. There is no contradiction between the two statements; rather, they complement each other by specifying different clinical contextsone based on the extent of the primary tumor and the other based on the presence of nodal or contiguous tissue involvement. Together, they define a broad population of cervical cancer patients for whom chemoradiation is the recommended standard of care.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="TREATMENT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemoradiation is a type of treatment for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
