      <data key="d8">COMPLETE REMISSION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chemotherapy is a specific type of systemic therapy. As a systemic therapy, chemotherapy is administered intravenously and travels through the bloodstream, allowing it to reach cells throughout the body rather than targeting only one localized area. This broad distribution is a defining characteristic of systemic treatment. Chemotherapy is also one of the most common types of systemic therapy used for bladder cancer. Thus, while systemic therapy is the broader category encompassing various treatment approaches that affect the whole body, chemotherapy is a prominent and widely utilized form of that category, particularly in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d8">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic therapy may be given first for advanced cancers or cancer that has spread to lymph nodes; metastasis indicates advanced/spread cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="SYSTEMIC CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic chemotherapy is a common type of systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">SYSTEMIC CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
