      <data key="d8">INFECTIONS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY" target="LYMPHOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">High-dose chemotherapy is given to kill lymphoma cells in the body</data>
      <data key="d8">LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVIVORSHIP" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">After cervical cancer treatment, patients enter survivorship and undergo surveillance for recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVIVORSHIP</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="THOM" target="MELISSA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both are anal cancer survivors quoted in the same document.</data>
      <data key="d8">MELISSA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="SURVIVORSHIP" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Survivorship care includes watching for symptoms of cervical cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVIVORSHIP</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cervical cancer can recur after treatment, and recurrence is a major focus of follow-up care.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="BIOMARKER TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biomarker testing plays a critical role in the management of recurrent cancers, helping to guide treatment decisions and identify potential therapeutic options. In the context of recurrent ovarian cancer, biomarker testing is used to help direct treatment by providing information that can inform which therapies are most likely to be effective for the individual patient. For recurrent cervical cancer, biomarker testing is specifically recommended for all patients with disease recurrence, as it serves to identify targeted therapy options or determine eligibility for clinical trials. Thus, across both recurrent ovarian and recurrent cervical cancers, biomarker testing is an essential tool for personalizing care after a recurrence, enabling clinicians to match patients with the most appropriate treatments, including targeted therapies, and to explore clinical trial opportunities when standard options are limited.</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="PHYSICAL EXAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A physical exam may first raise suspicion of recurrence because of symptoms or abnormal findings.</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICAL EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If recurrence is suspected, imaging tests such as CT, PET, or MRI are used to check for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Patients with persistent or recurrent ovarian cancer are encouraged to consider a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Anal cancer may return after a cancer-free period, which is called recurrence</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Recurrence can include metastasis of the cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="SURVEILLANCE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surveillance is conducted to monitor for recurrence or relapse.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="BLADDER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Bladder cancer commonly comes back after treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="BRACHYTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy may be given for recurrent cervical cancer, particularly with radical hysterectomy or pelvic radiation.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="BLOOD TEST" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Blood tests may be ordered if symptoms or a suspicious physical exam suggest recurrent cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If cancer returns to the anal area, abdominoperineal resection is recommended</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="PELVIC EXENTERATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pelvic exenteration is a surgical treatment option for cervical cancer that returns only to the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Radical hysterectomy may be used to treat recurrent cervical cancer when radiation is not an option and the cancer can be removed.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="RESECTION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Resection of new cancer growth is a treatment option for local/regional recurrence in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="COMPLETE REMISSION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer can return after complete remission; if it returns more than 6 months later, the disease is considered platinum-sensitive.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="CANCER PROGRESSION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cancer growth or spread is a form of disease recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="SECONDARY CYTOREDUCTIVE SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Secondary cytoreductive surgery may be recommended before treatment for recurrent disease in certain circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="EXPLORATORY SURGERY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Exploratory surgery may be performed to learn the extent of recurrent cervical cancer and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="PSMA-PET SCAN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Detection of recurrence commonly involves imaging such as PSMA-PET scans.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="DIAGNOSTIC TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Recurrence detection involves diagnostic tests including imaging and biomarker testing.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="PROGNOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The likelihood of recurrence is a key component of prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="FOLLOW-UP TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Follow-up testing is conducted to monitor for recurrence after bladder cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="BLOOD AND IMAGING TESTS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Blood and imaging tests may be ordered during surveillance when symptoms suggest possible relapse.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="SURVIVORSHIP" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Survivorship focuses on long-term side effects and healthy habits after colon cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVIVORSHIP</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="LYNCH SYNDROME" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the diseases onset.</data>
      <data key="d8">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Microsatellite instability testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">All colon cancers should be tested for mismatch repair mutations; mismatch repair testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited syndrome closely related to colon cancer. In classic FAP, the polyps characteristic of the condition can become invasive colon cancer, often by age 50. Thus, colon cancer is a major complication of this inherited syndrome, with FAP serving as a direct precursor condition.</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="ADENOMA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Adenomas can become invasive colon cancer over time</data>
      <data key="d8">ADENOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="POLYPS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Almost all colon cancers start in a polyp, and removing polyps can prevent colon cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">POLYPS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="COLORECTAL CANCER SCREENING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Colorectal cancer screening can detect colon cancer early, when it is most treatable.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLORECTAL CANCER SCREENING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="COLORECTAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Colon cancer is a colorectal cancer that forms in the colon.</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="SURVEILLANCE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surveillance is part of the post-treatment management for colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER SUPPORT COMMUNITY" target="NATIONAL COALITION FOR CANCER SURVIVORSHIP" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
