    <edge source="CYSTOSCOPY" target="BLUE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cystoscopy is a medical procedure that allows doctors to examine the inside of the bladder and urethra. Blue light cystoscopy is a newer, specialized type of cystoscopy that enhances this examination. It uses blue light and a photosensitizing dye, which causes abnormal tissue to appear differently under the blue light, aiding in the detection and evaluation of conditions such as bladder cancer. Together, cystoscopy and blue light cystoscopy represent standard and advanced approaches to urological diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYSTOSCOPY" target="NARROW-BAND IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Narrow-band imaging is used during cystoscopy to detect tumors more clearly</data>
      <data key="d8">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYSTOSCOPY" target="WHITE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">White light cystoscopy is the standard form of cystoscopy that uses white light</data>
      <data key="d8">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYSTOSCOPY" target="URETEROSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ureteroscopy is a very similar exam to cystoscopy but goes farther to examine the ureters and inner kidneys</data>
      <data key="d8">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYSTOSCOPY" target="BIOPSY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Biopsy can be done during cystoscopy using a small tool on the cystoscope to remove suspicious tissue; if not done then, it may be scheduled separately</data>
      <data key="d8">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYSTOSCOPY" target="IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If cystoscopy does not find cancer in the urethra or bladder, imaging tests are used to investigate the ureters, kidneys, or other areas</data>
      <data key="d8">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYSTOSCOPY" target="TESTING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cystoscopy is a testing procedure used to look for signs of cancer in the bladder</data>
      <data key="d8">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYSTOSCOPY" target="PROCTOSCOPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both are procedures used under anesthesia to examine organs for signs of cancer</data>
      <data key="d8">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYSTOSCOPY" target="TUMOR REMOVAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If a tumor is found during cystoscopy, it must be removed in an operating room; very small growths can be removed during cystoscopy</data>
      <data key="d8">CYSTOSCOPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRACHYTHERAPY" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Brachytherapy is used to treat cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="STAGING" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The M score in AJCC staging records whether metastasis has occurred.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GYNECOLOGIST" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Gynecologists may be reached out to for evaluation of anal symptoms</data>
      <data key="d8">GYNECOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) provides comprehensive clinical trial information and search resources. These resources are available through the NCI's official website at cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/clinical-trials/search, where patients, caregivers, and researchers can locate ongoing clinical trials. In addition to the online search tool, the National Cancer Institute's Cancer Information Service is also available to help individuals find clinical trials that may be relevant to their needs. Together, these offerings ensure that the public has access to both self-service search tools and personalized assistance when seeking information about clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) serves as a key resource for patients and others seeking information about clinical trials. As part of its mission, NCI provides a dedicated clinical-trials search tool on its official website, cancer.gov, enabling users to find relevant studies. In addition to this search functionality, NCI actively supports patients throughout the process of identifying and accessing clinical trials, reinforcing its role as a trusted resource in cancer research and care.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. government organization operating within the United States. It is formally a U.S. government agency and institute, and it is located in the United States. The NCI is also listed under "In the United States" as a resource for finding clinical trials, reflecting its role in providing access to cancer research and treatment information. As a government entity, the NCI operates as part of the U.S. federal structure, dedicated to cancer research and related public health efforts.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) and the U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) are both U.S. government health organizations and official resources for finding clinical trials. NCI provides cancer-specific clinical trial search tools, including access through cancer.gov, while NLM maintains ClinicalTrials.gov, a comprehensive database of clinical studies. Together, these two entities serve as key sources for patients, researchers, and the public seeking information on clinical trials, with NCI focusing on cancer-related trials and NLM offering a broader clinical trial registry. Both are listed as reliable, government-supported resources for clinical-trial searching.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The FDA and NCI are U.S. government agencies involved in cancer treatment and clinical research; the FDA approves treatments while NCI provides trial resources.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) operates the Cancer Information Service (CIS), which is an integral part of the Institute. The CIS functions as NCI's dedicated service for providing cancer information and clinical trial assistance. It helps both patients and the general public find relevant clinical trials and offers detailed information about clinical trial options. As a core component of the National Cancer Institute, the Cancer Information Service serves as a reliable resource for those seeking guidance on cancer-related topics and access to clinical trial information.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" target="NCI'S CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Cancer Information Service is part of the National Cancer Institute and helps people find clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. National Library of Medicine maintains ClinicalTrials.gov, a comprehensive worldwide clinical trial registry and search database. This resource is provided by the U.S. National Library of Medicine to help users find clinical trials. ClinicalTrials.gov serves as a global repository, allowing researchers, patients, and the public to locate and access information about clinical trials conducted around the world.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NLM operates clinicaltrials.gov, a database for clinical trials. NLM operates clinicaltrials.gov, a database for searching clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a U.S. federal institution and government organization operating within the United States. As a U.S. government library, it is explicitly identified as an American institution and serves as the national library of medicine for the country. In addition to its role as a federal library, the NLM is known for providing the ClinicalTrials.gov database, a key resource for clinical research information. The NLM operates under the broader framework of the United States, functioning as a U.S. government entity dedicated to medical and health information.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" target="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The FDA and NLM are U.S. government health-related agencies; NLM provides clinical trial data and FDA reviews treatments.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" target="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE (NLM) and the CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE (CIS) are both organizations that help people find clinical trials. NLM maintains ClinicalTrials.gov, a comprehensive online database of clinical studies, while CIS offers direct, personal assistance to individuals seeking information about clinical trial options. Together, they provide complementary support: NLM supplies the searchable public resource, and CIS delivers one-on-one guidance to help users navigate the process.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" target="WORLDWIDE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. National Library of Medicine's clinicaltrials.gov database is a worldwide resource for finding clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
