      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE (NCI)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN FOUNDATION" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The NCCN Foundation funds the nation's promising doctors at the center of innovation in cancer research.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN FOUNDATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN FOUNDATION" target="PFIZER INC." id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pfizer Inc. is a corporate supporter acknowledged by the NCCN Foundation for helping to make available the NCCN Guidelines for Patients. Pfizer Inc. is a corporate supporter acknowledged by the NCCN Foundation for helping make the NCCN Guidelines for Patients available.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN FOUNDATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Clinical trials offer participants access to investigational treatments that have not yet been fully approved. Over time, these treatments may receive approval from the U.S. FDA, making them more widely available. Thus, clinical trials serve as a pathway for patients to obtain emerging therapies while also generating data that can support eventual regulatory approval by the U.S. FDA.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A drug, device, or treatment approach found safe and effective in a clinical trial may be approved by the U.S. FDA.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal agency of the United States. It operates within the United States as a U.S. federal agency, fulfilling its regulatory and public health responsibilities under the authority of the U.S. government.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="PHASE 3 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If Phase 3 trial results are good, the treatment may be approved by the FDA.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="PHASE 4 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 4 trials study the long-term safety and benefit of an FDA-approved treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both are resources that help patients find clinical trials; NLM provides clinicaltrials.gov and CIS offers direct assistance.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Cancer Information Service assists individuals in locating appropriate clinical trials, including providing guidance on how to find them. It also answers cancer-related questions and addresses specific inquiries about clinical trials, such as their purpose, procedures, and eligibility. Additionally, the service offers contact information for further assistance, ensuring that individuals have access to the support they need when seeking information about cancer and clinical trial participation.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">CIS helps people find clinical trials and provides cancer information by phone</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Cancer Information Service is a U.S.-based entity that operates as part of the National Cancer Institute, which is also based in the United States. As a service of this institute, it is situated within the broader organizational framework of the U.S. National Cancer Institute, reflecting its role in providing cancer-related information and support within the United States.</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION" target="PEER-TO-PEER SUPPORT PROGRAM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Anal Cancer Foundation runs the Peer-To-Peer Support Program and provides contact for matching patients/caregivers with peer volunteers.</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The text lists the NCCN Guidelines and the Anal Cancer Foundations Peer-To-Peer Support Program as support resources for cancer survivors.</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION" target="THOM" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Thom is an anal cancer survivor quoted in the same survivorship resource that also presents Anal Cancer Foundation support services.</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER FOUNDATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PEER-TO-PEER SUPPORT PROGRAM" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both are referenced in the same patient-education material as resources for cancer support and survivorship.</data>
      <data key="d8">PEER-TO-PEER SUPPORT PROGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" target="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both are U.S. organizations involved in the clinical trial process: NCI helps patients find trials, and FDA approves treatments found safe and effective in trials</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) may approve a drug, device, or treatment approach after it has been found safe and effective in a clinical trial. Clinical trials are the settings in which investigational treatments are studied, and these treatments may later be approved by the FDA. In general, any treatment approach that demonstrates safety and effectiveness through clinical trial results is eligible for FDA approval. Thus, the FDA's approval process relies on clinical trial evidence to determine whether a drug, device, or treatment should be made available to patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal agency of the United States. It operates within the U.S. federal government and is responsible for protecting public health by regulating various products. Notably, the FDA may approve new treatments after they have undergone clinical trials, ensuring that medical products meet safety and effectiveness standards before they are made available to the public. As a U.S. federal agency, it plays a key role in the nation's healthcare and regulatory systems.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" target="PHASE 3 CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If Phase 3 trial results are good, the treatment may be approved by the FDA.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" target="PHASE 4 CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 4 trials study the long-term safety and benefit of an FDA-approved treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" target="U.S." id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The FDA is the U.S. regulatory agency named in the text.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" target="PHASE III TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">After positive Phase III trial results, a treatment may be approved by the FDA.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION" target="PHASE IV TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase IV trials study the long-term safety and benefits of an FDA-approved treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCI provides information on clinical trials that may eventually lead to FDA-approved treatments</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If a new drug, device, or treatment approach is found to be safe and effective in a clinical trial, it may be approved by the FDA. If a new drug, device, or treatment approach is found safe and effective in a clinical trial, it may be approved by the FDA.</data>
      <data key="d8">FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="UNITED STATES" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The FDA, or the U.S. Food and Drug Administration, is a federal agency of the United States. It serves as a U.S. regulatory agency responsible for approving cancer treatments, among its broader public health regulatory duties.</data>
      <data key="d8">FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="PHASE 3 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is the regulatory authority responsible for approving new treatments and therapies. Phase 3 trials are a critical stage in the clinical research process, designed to test a drug or treatment approach against a standard existing treatment to determine its effectiveness, safety, and overall benefit. If the results of a Phase 3 trial are favorablemeaning the new treatment demonstrates sufficient efficacy and an acceptable safety profilethe treatment approach may be submitted for approval by the FDA. Thus, successful Phase 3 trial outcomes are a key prerequisite for FDA approval, allowing the treatment to become available to patients.</data>
      <data key="d8">FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="PHASE 4 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and Phase 4 trials are closely related in the context of post-approval drug and treatment monitoring. Phase 4 trials are conducted after a treatment has already received FDA approval. These trials focus on studying the long-term safety and benefit of the FDA-approved treatment. By gathering additional data over an extended period, Phase 4 trials help confirm that the treatment continues to be safe and effective in real-world patient populations, beyond the controlled conditions of earlier clinical trial phases. This ongoing evaluation supports the FDA's broader mission of ensuring that approved medical products maintain a favorable risk-benefit profile once they are available to the public. In summary, Phase 4 trials serve as a crucial post-marketing surveillance mechanism, with the FDA overseeing and relying on their findings to monitor the long-term safety and benefit of approved treatments.</data>
      <data key="d8">FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="PHASE 2 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 2 trials test how well a drug works against a specific cancer, and positive results may contribute to later FDA approval.</data>
      <data key="d8">FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FDA" target="PHASE 1 TRIALS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phase 1 trials are the earliest phase of clinical trials, and the drug or approach tested may later be submitted for FDA approval if it is safe and effective.</data>
      <data key="d8">FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="METASTASIS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The AJCC TNM system assesses M (metastasis) to stage colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="TNM SCORING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The AJCC staging system uses TNM scoring to describe the extent of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) and the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) have both developed staging systems for ovarian cancer. The two systems are similar in structure and intent, providing standardized frameworks for classifying the extent of the disease. Although both staging systems are recognized and used in clinical practice, the FIGO system is employed most often. This reflects its widespread acceptance and practical utility in the management and communication of ovarian cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GALLBLADDER AND BILE DUCT TUMORS" target="LIVER CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Gallbladder and bile duct tumors are presented as related cancer topics alongside liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">LIVER CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="NCCN" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">NCCN provides patient guidelines and resources for people seeking ALL treatment.</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="B-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="T-ALL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature white blood cells called lymphoblasts. One of the two main types of ALL is T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL). T-ALL specifically originates in T-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell that plays a central role in the immune system. As one of the two primary subtypes of ALL, T-ALL is distinguished from the other main type, which arises from B-cell lymphocytes. Together, these two subtypes encompass the majority of acute lymphoblastic leukemia cases. T-ALL develops when genetic mutations or other alterations cause T-cell precursors to multiply uncontrollably, leading to an accumulation of abnormal cells in the bone marrow, blood, and sometimes other organs. The disease is aggressive and requires prompt treatment, typically involving chemotherapy, and in some cases targeted therapy or stem cell transplantation. Although T-ALL and B-ALL share many clinical features, they differ in their cellular origins, immunophenotypic markers, and certain molecular characteristics, which can influence treatment response and prognosis. T-ALL is more common in adolescents and young adults, while B-ALL is more frequent in younger children. Because T-ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, all general information about ALLincluding its causes, symptoms such as fatigue, fever, easy bruising, and bone pain, as well as diagnostic approaches like blood counts and bone marrow biopsyalso applies to T-ALL. However, specific management strategies for T-ALL may be adapted based on the distinct biology of T-cell disease. In summary, T-ALL is one of the two main forms of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, originating specifically from T-cell lymphocytes, and is a distinct yet closely related entity within the broader ALL classification.</data>
      <data key="d8">T-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d8">B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
