    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="RECURRENCE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surveillance is performed to detect cancer recurrence or new cancer growth early.</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="ANAL CANCER" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surveillance is performed to find early signs that anal cancer may have returned</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="IMAGING" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surveillance and imaging are closely intertwined in the management of anal cancer. Imaging is a recommended component of surveillance for patients with stage II and III anal cancers who have achieved complete remission. As part of post-treatment follow-up, imaging is also recommended every year for three years during surveillance after treatment for recurrence. Additionally, imaging is employed during surveillance when new or worsening symptoms arise that suggest possible recurrence or spread of disease. The surveillance process specifically includes imaging modalities such as endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI to monitor for disease status. In summary, imaging serves as a routine, scheduled tool in surveillance protocols, as well as a targeted diagnostic measure when clinical signs raise concern, with the choice of modality typically involving endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI.</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Surveillance is recommended after completing chemoradiation to check treatment results</data>
