    <edge source="COLECTOMY" target="HERNIA" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hernia is a possible short- or long-term side effect of colectomy, caused by organs pushing through weakened tissues.</data>
      <data key="d8">HERNIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="TURBT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical therapy and TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumor) are two treatments commonly used together for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d8">TURBT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical BCG therapy is a form of intravesical therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical chemotherapy is a type of intravesical therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="INTRAVESICAL BCG" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical BCG is a type of intravesical therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical therapy and chemotherapy are both cancer-fighting treatments, but they differ in how the drugs are administered. Intravesical therapy is a localized treatment used primarily for bladder cancer, in which medication is placed directly into the bladder through a catheter. Chemotherapy, in its common systemic form, involves cancer-fighting drugs given throughout the body by infusion, usually through a vein, so that the drugs travel in the bloodstream to reach cancer cells anywhere in the body. However, the two are closely related in the context of bladder cancer: chemotherapy is actually one of the two main intravesical therapies. This means that chemotherapy drugs can be administered intravesicallydirectly into the bladderrather than systemically. In that form, it is known as intravesical chemotherapy. The other main intravesical therapy is typically immunotherapy (such as BCG), which is not chemotherapy. Thus, the apparent contradiction is resolved by distinguishing between two modes of chemotherapy: intravesical chemotherapy, where the drugs are placed directly into the bladder as a type of intravesical therapy, and systemic chemotherapy, where the drugs are infused throughout the body. Both intravesical therapy and chemotherapy involve cancer-fighting drugs, but intravesical therapy is always local to the bladder, while chemotherapy can be either local (intravesical) or systemic.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical therapy is a local treatment, whereas systemic therapy treats the whole body; the text contrasts these approaches.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical chemotherapy may be used instead of intravesical BCG if BCG is unavailable or ineffective, and both may cause similar side effects.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If intravesical BCG doesn't work on its own, other treatment options are available in addition to treatment in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="MAINTENANCE THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Maintenance therapy may be continued after intravesical BCG therapy every 6 months for up to 3 years.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="BCG SHORTAGE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Because of the BCG shortage, many patients cannot receive intravesical BCG therapy or receive a reduced dose.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="BCG MAINTENANCE THERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical BCG therapy may be followed by BCG maintenance therapy.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" target="SYSTEMIC CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Systemic chemotherapy is whole-body therapy, while intravesical chemotherapy only affects the inside of the bladder; the text explicitly contrasts the two.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" target="MAINTENANCE INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Maintenance intravesical chemotherapy is a continuation of intravesical chemotherapy, usually given monthly for 1 to 2 years.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical chemotherapy is chemotherapy directed only into the bladder rather than the whole body.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" target="BCG SHORTAGE" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">If a regular course of BCG treatment isn't available due to shortage, intravesical chemotherapy may be offered instead.</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG" target="TURBT" id="0">
      <data key="d5">related_to</data>
      <data key="d6">1.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Intravesical BCG is usually started 3 to 6 weeks after TURBT and may be continued as maintenance after TURBT.</data>
